flushSync — Focus Management

Notes compiled from the official react.dev reference plus a survey of how other developers actually use this API in production. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What does flushSync do, and what’s its signature?
A: flushSync(callback) is a general-purpose escape hatch that forces every state update inside callback to be applied to the DOM synchronously, before the next line of code runs — bypassing React’s normal batching of updates. In our example, wrapping setText(&#039;&#039;) and setTodos(...) in flushSync guarantees the new &lt;li&gt; genuinely exists in the DOM by the time scrollIntoView runs on the next line.
import { flushSync } from &#039;react-dom&#039;;

flushSync(() =&gt; {
  setSomeState(newValue);
});
// DOM is guaranteed up to date here
Q: Why does React call it a “de-optimization”?
A: Because it forces a blocking, synchronous re-render (React can’t batch/schedule it optimally), can force pending Suspense boundaries to show their fallback, and may flush other pending updates unrelated to your callback along with it — this cost applies no matter what you’re using it for, not just in this note’s example. In our example, that trade-off is accepted because the alternative (a stale DOM read) breaks the scroll-to-new-item interaction entirely.
Q: When would you actually reach for it?
A: Only when something outside React needs to read the DOM synchronously right after your code finishes running — e.g. calling .focus() or .scrollIntoView() on a just-rendered element, reading DOM state inside a browser event like onbeforeprint, or handing off to a third-party DOM library — never as a general “make my update happen faster” tool. In our example, that “something outside React” is scrollIntoView, called on listRef.current.lastChild right after adding a todo.
Q: How is it different from useLayoutEffect?
A: Both solve “something needs the DOM updated before it reads it,” but flushSync is imperative — called directly inside an event handler to force one specific update through immediately — while useLayoutEffect runs automatically after every commit, before paint, for reading/reacting to layout. In our example, flushSync is used because the scroll happens inside the same click handler that triggered the state update, not in a separate effect reacting to it after the fact — see useLayoutEffect for the effect-based half of this same class of problem.
React normally batches state updates for performance — several setState calls in one handler produce one re-render, applied asynchronously relative to the code that triggered them. flushSync(callback) forces every update inside callback to be applied to the DOM synchronously, before the next line of code runs.
import { flushSync } from &#039;react-dom&#039;;

function handleAdd() {
  const newTodo = { id: nextId++, text };
  flushSync(() =&gt; {
    setText(&#039;&#039;);
    setTodos([...todos, newTodo]);
  });
  // By this line, the DOM already reflects the new todo —
  // the newly-rendered &lt;li&gt; genuinely exists to scroll to.
  listRef.current.lastChild.scrollIntoView({ behavior: &#039;smooth&#039;, block: &#039;nearest&#039; });
}

💬 Without flushSync here, listRef.current.lastChild would still be the previous last item — React hadn’t re-rendered yet, so the new &lt;li&gt; doesn’t exist in the DOM at the time scrollIntoView runs. This is exactly the shape of the focus-management problem this API is built for: conditionally-rendered UI (e.g. an input that only appears in “editing” mode) needs to actually exist in the DOM before .focus() can target it, and normal batching means it might not yet.

Why it’s called out as a “de-optimization”

It forces a synchronous, blocking re-render — used often, this hurts performance, since React can no longer batch/schedule that work optimally.
It can force pending Suspense boundaries to show their fallback state.
It may run pending Effects synchronously, and — to satisfy the callback — React may even flush other pending updates that had nothing to do with your callback.

Rule of thumb: reach for it only when integrating with something that reads the DOM synchronously right after your code runs (browser focus APIs, window.print()/onbeforeprint, third-party DOM libraries) — not as a general “make my update happen faster” tool.

Real-World Scenarios
Where other developers actually reach for flushSync:

Focus management on inline edit toggles. An EditableText component that swaps a &lt;span&gt; for an &lt;input&gt; on click: the input must exist in the DOM before .focus() is called on it, and without flushSync wrapping the state update that renders the input, the focus call fires on a stale (pre-update) DOM.
Print styling via onbeforeprint. The browser’s onbeforeprint event needs the page’s DOM already reflecting print-specific state (e.g. an isPrinting flag driving different CSS/layout) by the time the print dialog opens — flushSync inside the onbeforeprint handler guarantees that, where a normal setState would still be batched/pending when the dialog opens.
Scrolling to a just-added DOM node. As in the todo-list example above: any “add an item, then immediately scroll to it” interaction needs the new node to genuinely exist in the DOM first.
Third-party / non-React library integration generally. Any browser API or external library that reads the DOM synchronously right after a callback returns (rather than on its own next tick) needs React’s update flushed first, or it sees stale DOM.


💬 Every real-world case here is a variant of the same sentence: “something outside React needs to read the DOM the instant my code finishes running, and React’s normal batching would make that read happen too early.” If nothing reads the DOM synchronously right after your state update, you don’t need flushSync — see useLayoutEffect for the read-before-paint half of this same class of problem.

Sources

