Mastering Focus Management in React with flushSync — Epic React by Kent C. Dodds
More Than You Need to Know About ReactDOM.flushSync — JulesBlom.com

use and createContext — Context

Notes compiled from the official react.dev reference (use, createContext) plus a survey of how other developers actually use context in production. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What problem does React Context solve?
A: Prop drilling — passing a value down through many layers of components that don’t themselves need it, just to get it to a deeply nested descendant. Context lets any descendant read a value directly without it being threaded through every intermediate component’s props. In our example, ThemeContext lets Button read the current theme directly, without MyPage having to pass a theme prop through every layer in between.
const ThemeContext = createContext(null);

function MyPage() {
  return (
    &lt;ThemeContext value=&quot;dark&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Form /&gt;
    &lt;/ThemeContext&gt;
  );
}

function Button() {
  const theme = use(ThemeContext);
  return &lt;button className={theme} /&gt;;
}
Q: What’s the difference between use and useContext for reading context?
A: They read context the same way, but use can be called conditionally and inside loops/early returns, while useContext — like any other Hook — must be called unconditionally at the top of the component; use can also unwrap Promises directly, which is what powers Suspense-based data fetching (see The use Hook), while useContext only ever reads context. In our example, HorizontalRule calls use(ThemeContext) inside an if (show) block — something useContext could never do.
Q: When should you reach for context vs. a dedicated state library?
A: Context works best for values that are read widely but written rarely — auth user, theme, locale, feature flags. If a value changes often and many components read it, every consumer re-renders on each change, so a dedicated state library or a useSyncExternalStore-based store is the better fit for frequently-changing shared state. In our example, theme is exactly that “read widely, changes rarely” shape, which is why plain context is the right tool rather than overkill.
Q: How do you avoid consumers getting undefined/null if a Provider is missing?
A: Wrap the read in a custom hook that throws a descriptive error if the context value is null — a general pattern for “safe” context consumption, applicable to any context, not just this one — turning a silent bug into an immediate, clear error at the call site and narrowing the TypeScript type back from T | null to T. In our example, useSearchParamsContext throws &#039;useSearchParamsContext must be used within a SearchParamsProvider&#039; if called outside a SearchParamsProvider.
createContext declares what can be shared; a Provider supplies the value; use (or useContext) reads it in any descendant:
const ThemeContext = createContext(null); // default value used if no Provider is above

function MyPage() {
  return (
    &lt;ThemeContext value=&quot;dark&quot;&gt;
      &lt;Form /&gt;
    &lt;/ThemeContext&gt;
  );
}

function Button() {
  const theme = use(ThemeContext);
  return &lt;button className={theme} /&gt;;
}

💬 As of React 19, the Provider is written as &lt;ThemeContext value={...}&gt; directly — &lt;ThemeContext.Provider&gt; still works but is the legacy form.

use vs useContext
use is the modern, more flexible way to read a context. The key difference: use can be called conditionally and inside loops, while useContext (like every other Hook) must be called unconditionally at the top level of the component.
function HorizontalRule({ show }) {
  if (show) {
    // ✅ fine — `use` isn&#039;t subject to the Rules of Hooks the same way
    const theme = use(ThemeContext);
    return &lt;hr className={theme} /&gt;;
  }
  return false;
}

💬 This works because use isn’t technically a Hook in the classic sense — it’s a special form the compiler/runtime understands can “unwrap” a value on demand (context or a Promise, see below), rather than one that must run identically on every render.

use also reads Promises directly (this is what powers Suspense-based data fetching) — pass it a Promise and it suspends the component until the Promise resolves:
function Profile() {
  const userPromise = use(UserContext); // context holding a Promise
  const user = use(userPromise);        // unwraps the Promise itself
  return &lt;h1&gt;{user.name}&lt;/h1&gt;;
}
Custom hook wrapper with error handling
Building a custom hook around use/createContext gives consumers a clear error instead of undefined when the Provider is missing — the standard pattern for “safe” context consumption:
const SearchParamsContext = createContext(null);

function useSearchParamsContext() {
  const context = use(SearchParamsContext);
  if (context === null) {
    throw new Error(&#039;useSearchParamsContext must be used within a SearchParamsProvider&#039;);
  }
  return context;
}

💬 The if (!value) throw runtime check is necessary even in TypeScript, because createContext(null) types the value as T | null — the check is what narrows it back to T for every consumer, and it turns a silent undefined bug into an immediate, descriptive error at the call site.


Real-World Scenarios
Where other developers actually reach for context:

Authentication. The single most common real-world context: store the current user (or null) plus login/logout functions, provide it near the app root, and read it anywhere with a useAuth()-style custom hook.
Theme (light/dark mode). The textbook example, and a genuinely common production one — a value that’s truly global and rarely changes, which is exactly the profile context is good at.
Internationalization / multi-language support. Large e-commerce apps use a locale context so any component can read the current language/strings without threading a locale prop through every layer.
Any other “global-ish” data: current theme, current user, current locale — anything that’s read in many unrelated places but only written in one.


💬 Notice the pattern across all of these: context works best for values that are read widely but written rarely. If a value changes often (e.g. on every keystroke) and many components read it, context re-renders every consumer on each change — that’s when a dedicated state library or useSyncExternalStore-based store is the better fit, not context.

Sources

Say Goodbye to Prop Drilling! Learn useContext in React Like a Pro — DEV Community
useContext in React: Solving Prop Drilling with Real Examples — react.wiki
Stop Prop Drilling with React’s useContext Hook &amp; TypeScript — Medium

useImperativeHandle — Imperative Handles

Notes compiled from the official react.dev reference (useImperativeHandle, forwardRef) plus a survey of how other developers actually use this hook in production. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What does useImperativeHandle do, and what’s its signature?
A: useImperativeHandle(ref, createHandle, deps) lets a component customize the value exposed on its ref, instead of exposing the raw underlying DOM node — a general encapsulation mechanism, the same idea as a class exposing a curated public API instead of its raw internals. In our example, MyInput uses it so the parent can only call .focus()/.scrollIntoView() on the ref, not reach into the raw &lt;input&gt; DOM node (e.g. .style is inaccessible).
useImperativeHandle(ref, () =&gt; ({
  focus() { inputRef.current.focus(); },
}), []);
Q: Why not just model this with state and props instead?
A: State is for data that should affect rendering; imperative actions like “focus this now” or “scroll to this now” are one-shot commands, not facts the UI should keep reflecting — this distinction holds for any imperative interaction, not just focus. Faking a command with state breaks in real ways — repeated triggers can be no-ops, updates aren’t synchronous (render → commit → effect), and triggering the same action from multiple places forces awkward event-counter hacks. In our example, a shouldFocus boolean prop fails exactly this way: clicking “Focus input” twice in a row only works the first time, since the second setShouldFocus(true) is a no-op on already-true state.
Q: What’s the actual signal that you need this hook?
A: Ask “does this change what’s rendered?” — if yes, use state/props; if you just need to imperatively tell a child to do something once from an event handler (open, close, scroll, shake, validate), that’s the gap useImperativeHandle fills, regardless of domain. In our example, “focus the input on button click” and “validate/reset a form on demand” are both commands, not rendering facts, which is why both reach for this hook instead of props.
Q: Where do real libraries use this pattern?
A: react-hook-form (trigger(), setFocus()), Framer Motion (animation.start()/.stop()), react-player (seekTo()), toast libraries’ imperative dismiss handles, and chart wrappers (exportAsImage()) — anywhere a command needs to be sent to an underlying instance rather than derived from a prop change. In our example, the modal-control and chat-scroll cases (open()/close(), scrollToBottom()) are the same shape as seekTo() or trigger() — a parent invoking a specific action on a child at a specific moment.
By default, forwarding a ref into a component exposes the raw underlying DOM node. useImperativeHandle lets the component author replace that with a custom object — exposing only the specific imperative methods you choose, and nothing else:
function MyInput({ ref, ...props }) {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () =&gt; ({
    // Only expose focus — nothing else
    focus() {
      inputRef.current.focus();
    },
    scrollIntoView() {
      inputRef.current.scrollIntoView();
    },
  }), []);

  return &lt;input {...props} ref={inputRef} /&gt;;
}
function Form() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  function handleClick() {
    inputRef.current.focus(); // ✅ works — focus was exposed
    // inputRef.current.style.opacity = 0.5; // ❌ fails — DOM node wasn&#039;t exposed
  }

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;MyInput ref={inputRef} /&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={handleClick}&gt;Focus the input&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

💬 This is an intentional encapsulation boundary, not just a convenience: the parent can call .focus() but can’t reach into .style, .value, or any other DOM property/method you didn’t explicitly list. Treat it like a public API surface for a component that otherwise hides its internals.

Composing imperative handles through nested components
The handle you expose can itself call imperative methods on child refs — letting a top-level button, say, scroll a comment list into view and focus the “add comment” input in one call:
function Post({ ref }) {
  const commentsRef = useRef(null);
  const addCommentRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () =&gt; ({
    scrollAndFocusAddComment() {
      commentsRef.current.scrollToBottom();
      addCommentRef.current.focus();
    },
  }), []);

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;CommentList ref={commentsRef} /&gt;
      &lt;AddComment ref={addCommentRef} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

Why not just use state + a prop?
State is for data that should affect rendering. Some interactions aren’t that — they’re one-shot commands (“focus this now”, “scroll to this now”) that don’t correspond to a fact the UI should keep reflecting. Trying to model a command as state forces you to invent state whose only job is to fake a method call.
Take “focus the input on button click,” built with only state:
function Parent() {
  const [shouldFocus, setShouldFocus] = useState(false);

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setShouldFocus(true)}&gt;Focus input&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;FancyInput shouldFocus={shouldFocus} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

function FancyInput({ shouldFocus }) {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (shouldFocus) inputRef.current.focus();
  }, [shouldFocus]);

  return &lt;input ref={inputRef} /&gt;;
}
This breaks in predictable ways:

Repeated triggers don’t work. Click the button twice — shouldFocus is already true, so the second setShouldFocus(true) is a no-op, the effect doesn’t re-run, nothing happens. You’d need to reset the flag somewhere, turning “focus the input” into a stateful lifecycle you have to manage.
It’s not synchronous. setShouldFocus(true) schedules a render → commit → effect. If the parent needs to do focusInput(); scrollToInput(); in a specific order as one action, it can’t — each step is a separate render cycle.
It doesn’t compose. Triggering focus from three different places (a button, a keyboard shortcut, a failed validation) means either three flags or an event-counter hack (setFocusToken(t =&gt; t + 1)) just to force the effect to fire again on an unchanged value.

useImperativeHandle replaces all of that with a direct method call — no extra state, no reset logic, no effect indirection:
function FancyInput({ ref, ...props }) {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () =&gt; ({
    focus() {
      inputRef.current.focus();
    },
  }), []);

  return &lt;input ref={inputRef} {...props} /&gt;;
}

function Parent() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);
  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; inputRef.current.focus()}&gt;Focus input&lt;/button&gt;
      &lt;FancyInput ref={inputRef} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}

💬 Rule of thumb: “does this change what’s rendered?” → state + props. “Do I just need to tell the child to do something once, imperatively, from an event handler?” → that’s the gap this hook fills. It’s the same category as calling .focus() or .play() on a raw DOM node, except the “node” is a component instance with a curated API instead of the full DOM surface.

A second example, showing state and imperative methods coexisting — useImperativeHandle doesn’t replace state, it adds an on-demand entry point into it:
function ContactForm({ ref }) {
  const [name, setName] = useState(&#039;&#039;);
  const [email, setEmail] = useState(&#039;&#039;);
  const nameRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () =&gt; ({
    reset() {
      setName(&#039;&#039;);
      setEmail(&#039;&#039;);
    },
    validate() {
      if (!email.includes(&#039;@&#039;)) {
        nameRef.current.focus();
        return false;
      }
      return true;
    },
  }), [email]);

  return (
    &lt;form&gt;
      &lt;input ref={nameRef} value={name} onChange={(e) =&gt; setName(e.target.value)} /&gt;
      &lt;input value={email} onChange={(e) =&gt; setEmail(e.target.value)} /&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  );
}

function Wizard() {
  const formRef = useRef(null);

  function handleNext() {
    if (formRef.current.validate()) goToNextStep();
  }

  function handleCancel() {
    formRef.current.reset();
  }

  return &lt;ContactForm ref={formRef} /&gt;;
}
name and email are still ordinary state driving rendering. What the imperative handle adds is a narrow, deliberate surface (reset, validate) the parent can call on demand — without the parent needing mirror state of its own, and without exposing the raw setters (the parent can’t do formRef.current.setName(...), only what’s explicitly listed). Same encapsulation point as the focus-only example above, just with internal state behind the exposed methods instead of a plain DOM node.
Real-World Scenarios
Where other developers actually reach for useImperativeHandle:

Third-party library integration. Libraries that need direct, imperative DOM access — e.g. Lottie (play/pause an animation) or Three.js (control a canvas scene) — get wrapped in a React component that exposes just the handful of imperative methods (play(), reset()) the rest of the app actually needs, instead of leaking the whole library instance.
Modal control. Exposing open() and close() methods on a modal component’s ref, so a parent can imperatively trigger it without prop-drilling an isOpen boolean and setter down, or standing up global state just to toggle visibility.
Custom form components. Exposing reset(), validate(), or focus() on a form or field component — the parent can trigger these without needing to manage the form’s internal state directly.
Chat/list UI effects. Exposing a scrollToBottom() method on a message list, or a “shake” animation trigger on an input to draw attention to a validation error — imperative, one-off UI effects that don’t map cleanly to a prop/state change.

Where popular libraries use this exact pattern

react-hook-form — formRef.current.trigger() validates on demand and setFocus(&#039;fieldName&#039;) jumps focus; “validate right now, tell me the result” isn’t a state change, it’s a command.
Framer Motion — imperative animation controls (useAnimate, animation.start() / .stop()) let you kick off an animation from an event handler rather than deriving it from a prop diff.
react-player and similar video/audio wrappers — playerRef.current.seekTo(30), .getCurrentTime(). Seeking is a command sent to the underlying &lt;video&gt; element, not a fact the UI re-renders to reflect.
Toast libraries (e.g. notistack) — closeSnackbar(key) / imperative dismiss handles, often needed because toasts render through a portal outside the normal prop-passing subtree.
react-select and combobox-style components — selectRef.current.focus(), .clearValue(), exposed alongside normal value/onChange props.
Chart wrappers (e.g. around Chart.js, Recharts) — chartRef.current.exportAsImage() or .resetZoom() — a one-off export/action, not something that should live in render-driving state.


💬 The pattern across all of these: the parent needs to tell a child component to do something at a specific moment (open now, scroll now, shake now) rather than describe an ongoing state the child should reflect. That’s the actual signal for useImperativeHandle — if you can express the interaction as “this prop’s value determines what’s shown,” you don’t need it; if you need “make this happen right now,” you do.

Sources

