useLayoutEffect — Layout Computations

Notes compiled from the official react.dev reference plus a survey of how other developers actually use this hook in production. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What’s the difference between useLayoutEffect and useEffect, and what’s the signature?
A: Same signature — useLayoutEffect(setup, deps) — but useLayoutEffect runs synchronously after the DOM updates and before the browser paints, while useEffect runs after paint; this timing distinction applies to any effect, not just layout measurement. In our example, Tooltip uses useLayoutEffect specifically because it measures getBoundingClientRect() and feeds that measurement into a re-render before the user sees anything.
useLayoutEffect(() =&gt; {
  const { height } = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();
  setTooltipHeight(height);
}, []);
Q: When would using useEffect instead cause a bug?
A: Any time an effect reads layout (e.g. getBoundingClientRect()) and calls setState to reposition or resize something — useEffect lets the browser paint the wrong value first, then a corrected render flashes in right after, a visible flicker, regardless of what’s being measured. In our example, that would mean the tooltip briefly rendering at height 0 before snapping to its real measured height.
Q: What’s the rule of thumb for choosing between them?
A: Default to useEffect always; only reach for useLayoutEffect when a visible layout flash is the actual symptom you’re fixing, since it blocks paint and overusing it can hurt perceived performance across the whole app. In our example, the tooltip, carousel, and chat-scroll cases all share that one symptom — visible jump/flicker — which is the actual trigger for reaching for useLayoutEffect, not “it measures the DOM” alone.
Q: Give a concrete example where it’s needed.
A: Any “measure → compute → apply, where apply changes what’s on screen” scenario — a tooltip positioning itself relative to a trigger, a carousel computing how many items fit a measured container width, or a chat list scrolling to the bottom after new messages arrive. In our example specifically, it’s a tooltip measuring available space around its trigger to decide whether to render above or below it, with the measurement and repositioning happening before paint so it never visibly jumps.
Same signature as useEffect, but React guarantees useLayoutEffect runs synchronously, after the DOM is updated but before the browser paints. Use it when an effect reads layout (e.g. getBoundingClientRect()) and needs to write state that affects what’s painted — otherwise the user briefly sees the wrong position/size flash by.
function Tooltip() {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  const [tooltipHeight, setTooltipHeight] = useState(0); // real height unknown yet

  useLayoutEffect(() =&gt; {
    const { height } = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();
    setTooltipHeight(height); // triggers a synchronous re-render before paint
  }, []);

  // ...use tooltipHeight to position the tooltip...
}
Why not just useEffect?
useEffect does not block the browser from painting — it runs after paint. If it measures layout and calls setState, the browser has already shown the wrong position once, then a second, corrected render flashes in right after: visible flicker.
// ❌ measures + repositions after paint → visible flicker
useEffect(() =&gt; {
  const { height } = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();
  setTooltipHeight(height);
}, []);

// ✅ measures + repositions before paint → no flicker
useLayoutEffect(() =&gt; {
  const { height } = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect();
  setTooltipHeight(height);
}, []);

💬 This maps directly onto the classic tooltip-positioning exercise: the client makes layout calculations to place a tooltip, and using useLayoutEffect instead of useEffect is the difference between the tooltip appearing correctly positioned on first paint vs. visibly jumping into place.

Rule of thumb: default to useEffect; reach for useLayoutEffect only when a visible layout flash is the actual symptom you’re fixing. It blocks paint, so overusing it can hurt perceived performance.

Real-World Scenarios
Where other developers report they actually needed useLayoutEffect (not just could use it):

Dynamically positioned tooltips/popovers. When a tooltip’s position depends on measuring available space around the trigger element (e.g. “show above if there’s room, otherwise show below”), the measurement and the repositioning have to happen before paint, or the tooltip visibly jumps from a wrong position to the right one.
Carousel item-count calculation from container width. One developer needed a fixed card width (e.g. 296px) with a dynamic number of visible items, computed by dividing the measured container width by the card width + gutter — done in useLayoutEffect so the carousel never renders with the wrong item count first.
Chat scroll-to-bottom on new messages. Scrolling a message list to the bottom after new messages are fetched: doing this in useEffect produced a visible flash/jump, while useLayoutEffect scrolled smoothly because the scroll position was corrected before the browser painted the new messages.


💬 All three examples share the same shape: measure → compute → apply, and the “apply” step changes what’s on screen. If your effect doesn’t feed back into what gets painted (e.g. it just logs something or fires an analytics call), there’s never a reason to reach for useLayoutEffect over useEffect.

