useEffect vs useLayoutEffect in React With Simple Examples and Real-Life Scenarios — Medium

useReducer — Complex State Management

Notes compiled from the official react.dev reference plus a survey of how other developers actually use this hook in production. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What does useReducer do differently from useState, and what’s its signature?
A: const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState) — instead of calling several setX functions scattered across event handlers, you dispatch a plain action object describing what happened, and one pure reducer function decides how state changes in response, centralizing update logic in one place for any state shape, not just lists. In our example, handleAddTask/handleChangeTask/handleDeleteTask all just dispatch an action; tasksReducer alone owns how the tasks array actually changes.
const [tasks, dispatch] = useReducer(tasksReducer, initialTasks);
dispatch({ type: &#039;added&#039;, id: nextId++, text });
Q: When should you reach for useReducer over useState?
A: Any time several pieces of state need to change together as one atomic transition — the general rule is “these values must move together,” not “this state happens to be complex.” In our example that’s a task list’s add/change/delete operations, but the same reasoning applies to a shopping cart or a form field updating in lockstep with its validation error.
Q: Why is defining the reducer outside the component useful?
A: The reducer is a plain, pure (state, action) =&gt; newState function — true of any reducer, regardless of domain — so it can be unit-tested directly without rendering anything: no component, no hooks, just call it with a state and an action and assert on the result. In our example, you could call tasksReducer(initialTasks, { type: &#039;added&#039;, id: 1, text: &#039;test&#039; }) directly in a test with no React involved at all.
Q: How do you get type safety out of a reducer’s actions?
A: Model the action as a discriminated union — a general TypeScript pattern for “one of several shapes, tagged by a field” — and TypeScript narrows action inside each switch case automatically, giving autocomplete only on the fields that exist for that action type. In our example, { type: &#039;reset&#039; } | { type: &#039;setCount&#039;; value: number } means action.value is only accessible inside the &#039;setCount&#039; case.
useReducer takes a reducer function and an initial state, and returns the current state plus a dispatch function:
const [tasks, dispatch] = useReducer(tasksReducer, initialTasks);
Instead of calling several setX functions scattered across event handlers, you dispatch a plain object describing what happened (an “action”), and one pure reducer function decides how state changes in response:
function handleAddTask(text) {
  dispatch({ type: &#039;added&#039;, id: nextId++, text });
}
function handleChangeTask(task) {
  dispatch({ type: &#039;changed&#039;, task });
}
function handleDeleteTask(taskId) {
  dispatch({ type: &#039;deleted&#039;, id: taskId });
}

function tasksReducer(tasks, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case &#039;added&#039;:
      return [...tasks, { id: action.id, text: action.text, done: false }];
    case &#039;changed&#039;:
      return tasks.map((t) =&gt; (t.id === action.task.id ? action.task : t));
    case &#039;deleted&#039;:
      return tasks.filter((t) =&gt; t.id !== action.id);
    default:
      throw Error(&#039;Unknown action: &#039; + action.type);
  }
}

💬 See useState vs useReducer for the deep-dive on when to reach for this over useState — short version: independent state stays in useState; state where the next value depends on another piece of state (so they change together) belongs in a reducer.

Typed with TypeScript
The reducer is a plain, pure (state, action) =&gt; newState function defined outside the component — so it’s unit-testable without rendering anything:
interface State { count: number }
type CounterAction = { type: &#039;reset&#039; } | { type: &#039;setCount&#039;; value: State[&#039;count&#039;] };

const initialState: State = { count: 0 };

function stateReducer(state: State, action: CounterAction): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case &#039;reset&#039;:
      return initialState;
    case &#039;setCount&#039;:
      return { ...state, count: action.value };
    default:
      throw new Error(&#039;Unknown action&#039;);
  }
}

💬 A discriminated union ({ type: &#039;reset&#039; } | { type: &#039;setCount&#039;; value: ... }) means TypeScript narrows action inside each case automatically — you get autocomplete on action.value only in the branch where it exists.

Sharing the logic via a custom hook
Wrapping useReducer in a custom hook (e.g. useTasks()) is how you make this logic reusable across components — the reducer, dispatch, and derived state all live behind one import, matching the course’s framing of “consolidate sprawling logic into a single place with a nice API.”

Real-World Scenarios
Where other developers actually reach for useReducer instead of useState:

Shopping carts / list state with multiple operations. Add, remove, and clear operations on an array collapse into one reducer instead of three separate useState calls and three handler functions each duplicating the “find and update” logic.
Complex forms. Login/signup or multi-field forms where several related fields and their validation state need to update together — a reducer keeps “set field X” and “set error for X” as one atomic transition instead of risking the two falling out of sync across renders.
State that outgrows a handful of independent flags. Once a component accumulates enough interrelated useState calls that a single user action needs to update three or four of them consistently, a reducer becomes the place that guarantees they move together — this is the general trigger developers report reaching for it once useState calls start to sprawl.


💬 Notice these all fall out of the same rule from useState vs useReducer: it’s never “useReducer is fancier,” it’s specifically “these values must change together, so encode that as one transition, not several.”

