useSyncExternalStore — Sync External Store

Notes compiled from the official react.dev reference plus a survey of how other developers and libraries actually use this hook in production. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What problem does useSyncExternalStore solve?
A: Subscribing to state that lives outside React (a browser API, a third-party store, a module-level variable) using useEffect + useState is prone to subtle bugs — like tearing under concurrent rendering or missed updates — for any kind of external data source, not just one specific API. In our example, that external source is navigator.onLine, which changes via browser online/offline events React has no visibility into on its own.
Q: What are its three arguments, and what’s the signature?
A: useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, getSnapshot, getServerSnapshot?) — subscribe registers a callback with the store and returns an unsubscribe function (must be a stable reference), getSnapshot returns the current immutable value, and the optional getServerSnapshot supplies the value used during SSR/hydration. In our example, subscribe wires up window.addEventListener(&#039;online&#039;/&#039;offline&#039;, ...), and getSnapshot just returns navigator.onLine.
useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, getSnapshot, getServerSnapshot);
Q: What happens if getSnapshot returns a new object/array on every call?
A: React thinks the value changed on every render and re-renders in a loop — this applies to any store, not just this example — so getSnapshot must return the store’s own immutable data directly, never a freshly computed object/array each call. In our example, returning navigator.onLine directly is safe because it’s a primitive boolean, not a freshly-constructed object.
Q: Why does getServerSnapshot matter for SSR?
A: The server snapshot and the first client render’s snapshot must match for any hook using this pattern, or React logs a hydration mismatch; if omitted, the component throws when rendered on the server. In our example, navigator.onLine doesn’t exist on the server, so getServerSnapshot hardcodes true as a reasonable default, and the client re-syncs to the real value after hydration.
Q: Do most developers call this hook directly?
A: Rarely — libraries like Zustand, Redux, and Jotai use it internally to bridge their external store with React’s concurrent rendering. Most app code just uses the library’s hook (e.g. Zustand’s useStore) rather than calling useSyncExternalStore by hand, though it’s a reasonable choice for a small, app-specific external store.
For state that lives outside React (browser APIs, a third-party store, a module-level variable) — subscribing “manually” with useEffect + useState is a well-known source of subtle bugs (tearing under concurrent rendering, missed updates). useSyncExternalStore is the hook built specifically for this:
function subscribe(callback) {
  window.addEventListener(&#039;online&#039;, callback);
  window.addEventListener(&#039;offline&#039;, callback);
  return () =&gt; {
    window.removeEventListener(&#039;online&#039;, callback);
    window.removeEventListener(&#039;offline&#039;, callback);
  };
}

function useOnlineStatus() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,          // how to subscribe — React only resubscribes if this changes
    () =&gt; navigator.onLine,   // getSnapshot: current value on the client
    () =&gt; true,                // getServerSnapshot: value during SSR
  );
}
Signature: useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, getSnapshot, getServerSnapshot?)

subscribe — registers a callback with the store and returns an unsubscribe function. Must be a stable reference (e.g. defined outside the component, or memoized) or React will resubscribe every render.
getSnapshot — returns the current value. Must return immutable data — return the store’s value directly rather than computing a fresh object/array each call, or React will think the value changed on every render and re-render in a loop.
getServerSnapshot (optional) — the value to use during server rendering and client hydration. If omitted, the component throws when rendered on the server — omit it only when you deliberately want this hook’s data to be client-only.

// ✅ correct — returns the store&#039;s own immutable data
function getSnapshot() {
  return myStore.todos;
}

💬 This is the piece that matters for SSR: the server snapshot and the first client render’s snapshot must match, otherwise React logs a hydration mismatch. navigator.onLine doesn’t exist on the server, so getServerSnapshot hardcodes true as a reasonable default rather than crashing — the client then re-syncs to the real value on the first effect pass after hydration.


Real-World Scenarios
Where other developers and libraries actually reach for useSyncExternalStore:

State management library internals. Modern versions of Zustand, Redux, and Jotai use useSyncExternalStore internally to bridge their own external store with React’s concurrent rendering — in Zustand specifically, it powers the useStore hook, subscribing to the vanilla store and ensuring every component reads the latest state without tearing. Most application developers never call the hook directly here — the library does it for them.
Reading localStorage reactively. Syncing a component’s state with a localStorage value that might change from another tab/window (via the storage event) is a textbook direct use — subscribe listens for the storage event, getSnapshot reads the current value.
Browser online/offline status. The canonical example from the React docs itself (navigator.onLine + the online/offline window events) shows up repeatedly as the simplest real illustration of the pattern.
Any non-React-aware external system: a vanilla JS third-party library’s internal state, a module-level global variable, or a Web Worker’s postMessage-driven state — anywhere React needs to read a value it doesn’t own and re-render when that value changes elsewhere.


💬 The practical takeaway developers converge on: if you’re reaching for a state library (Zustand, Jotai, etc.) specifically to get “safe subscription to external state,” you may not need the library at all — useSyncExternalStore plus a small custom store is often described as “all you need” for a simple external-store use case. Reach for the hook directly when the store is small and app-specific; reach for a library when you need its ecosystem (devtools, middleware, selectors) on top.

