Custom Components

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.


“Components are functions which accept an object called ‘props’ and return something that is renderable.” — Kent C. Dodds

That’s the entire definition. Nothing else is special about a component. This exercise builds up to that definition iteratively, in four steps, to show exactly how JSX and createElement treat a function differently from a string.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What is a React component, really?
A: At its core, any plain JavaScript function that accepts a single object argument (props) and returns something renderable (JSX, a string, null, an array, etc.) qualifies as a component — there’s no class requirement, no special syntax, no inheritance. React’s only real requirement is that it controls when that function gets called, not you. In our example, message/Message is exactly this: a function taking props and returning a &lt;div&gt;.
Q: What’s the difference between calling a function directly versus passing it to createElement (or using it in JSX)?
A: This distinction matters for every component, not just this example: calling a function directly (message({...})) is an ordinary JS call — React never receives a reference to it, so it can’t decide when to invoke it, skip re-invoking it, or later attach hooks/state to it. Handing the function itself to createElement/JSX instead gives React that reference, so React owns the scheduling of the call — which is the mechanism that makes hooks, reconciliation, and lazy rendering possible at all. In our example, message({ children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }) runs immediately as a plain call, while createElement(message, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }) lets React decide when message actually executes.
message({ children: &#039;Hi&#039; })              // plain call — React has no say in this
createElement(message, { children: &#039;Hi&#039; }) // reference — React controls invocation
Q: Why must custom component names be capitalized in JSX?
A: This is a JSX/Babel compilation rule that applies to any component, anywhere: Babel decides whether a tag name compiles to a string (createElement(&#039;div&#039;, ...)) or a variable reference (createElement(Message, ...)) purely from the tag’s casing — lowercase/kebab-case tags always become strings meant to match native HTML elements, while capitalized (or property-access) tags become references to your component. In our example, renaming message to Message and writing &lt;Message&gt; (instead of &lt;message&gt;) is what makes Babel treat it as a component reference rather than an attempt to render a nonexistent &lt;message&gt; HTML element.
Q: Is there anything special about the children prop?
A: No — across all of React, children is just a regular prop that JSX gives special syntax sugar for nesting content between tags; under the hood it’s passed exactly like any other prop. A component can define and accept any prop names, primitives, objects, or other elements — children isn’t reserved or magic. In our example, &lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt; compiles to the same thing as &lt;Message children=&quot;Hello World&quot; /&gt;.
Q: Does a component’s function body persist state between renders?
A: No, and this is universal to all function components: React re-invokes the entire function from scratch on every render, so any plain local variable (let, const) is reinitialized every time and can’t “remember” anything across renders on its own. This is precisely the problem hooks like useState/useRef exist to solve — they let React persist a value across a function’s re-invocations by tracking it outside the function body. In our example, a let declared inside Calculator would reset on every render; nothing in this exercise persists state yet since hooks haven’t been introduced.
Simple Function

Lesson

function message(props) {
  return &lt;div className=&quot;message&quot;&gt;{props.children}&lt;/div&gt;
}

const element = (
  &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
    {message({ children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })}
    {message({ children: &#039;Goodbye World&#039; })}
  &lt;/div&gt;
)

💬 This reduces duplication, but it is not a real React component yet — it’s just a plain function being called directly and interpolated. The distinction matters: React never gets a reference to message, so it can’t defer calling it, track it, or later attach state/effects to it. That’s the gap the next two steps close.

Raw API

Lesson

const element = &lt;div&gt;{createElement(message, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })}&lt;/div&gt;

💬 Passing message itself (not calling it) as the first argument to createElement is the semantic shift: React now owns calling this function, and defers doing so until it’s actually necessary to render. Console-logging inside message before and after this change shows the calls happening in a different order — proof that React, not your code, now controls when the function runs. Children passed this way show up as props.children (as an array, if there’s more than one).

JSX Components

Lesson

function Message(props) {
  return &lt;div className=&quot;message&quot;&gt;{props.children}&lt;/div&gt;
}

const element = &lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt;

💬 Babel decides whether JSX tag names compile to a string (createElement(&#039;message&#039;, ...)) or a reference (createElement(message, ...)) purely from the tag’s casing/shape: capitalized (&lt;Capitalized&gt;) and property-access (&lt;foo.Bar&gt;) tags compile to references; lowercase and kebab-case tags compile to strings. Since there’s no native &lt;message&gt; DOM element, &lt;message&gt; would throw a “did you mean to render a component?” warning. Convention: capitalize custom component names.

Props

Lesson

function Calculator({ left, operator, right }) {
  const result = operations[operator](left, right)
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;code&gt;
        {left} {operator} {right} = &lt;output&gt;{result}&lt;/output&gt;
      &lt;/code&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

// &lt;Calculator left={1} operator=&quot;+&quot; right={2} /&gt;

💬 Props are entirely up to the component author — there’s nothing special about children beyond JSX’s implicit syntax for it (&lt;Message&gt;x&lt;/Message&gt; ≡ &lt;Message children=&quot;x&quot; /&gt;). A component can accept any prop names, primitives, objects, or other React elements. Think of a component as a function and JSX usage as a function call — React just controls when that “call” actually happens.


💬 One implication worth internalizing early: React re-invokes a component’s function on every render, from scratch — it isn’t called once and kept alive. A let variable declared inside Calculator gets reset to its initial value every single time. Nothing persists between calls yet, which is exactly the gap useState (from the React Hooks workshop) exists to fill. Also, because a component is just a value, you can choose one dynamically like any other variable: const Panel = isAdmin ? AdminPanel : UserPanel then &lt;Panel /&gt; — no special API needed for that. (In real codebases, the PascalCase convention itself is usually enforced by eslint-plugin-react’s naming rules rather than left to memory.)

Error Boundaries

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

An unhandled error during render removes your entire app from the page, leaving a blank screen. You can’t just wrap JSX in try/catch because — as seen in Custom Components — JSX only describes elements; the component functions aren’t actually called until React gets around to rendering them, at a point your try/catch has already exited. The fix is a special component category called an Error Boundary, used from the third-party react-error-boundary package (Kent is a maintainer) rather than hand-rolled, since Error Boundaries can currently only be implemented as class components.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why can’t you just use try/catch around JSX to handle render errors?
A: This is true of any framework built on a virtual DOM, not just this example: JSX only describes elements as data — the actual component functions aren’t called until React later walks that tree during its own render pass, well after your try/catch block has already exited. Error Boundaries exist specifically as a React-level mechanism to catch errors thrown during React’s own render process, which a JS-level try/catch structurally can’t reach. In our example, wrapping &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt; in JSX with a surrounding try/catch wouldn’t catch anything, because OnboardingForm isn’t actually invoked until React renders it.
Q: Why does the component that might throw need to be a child of the ErrorBoundary, not merged into the same component?
A: This is a general rule for any error-boundary implementation, in any UI framework that has the concept: a boundary can only catch errors from components strictly beneath it in the tree — it can’t catch an error that occurs while React is still constructing the boundary’s own surrounding tree, since the boundary hasn’t been “seen” yet at that point. In our example, that’s why OnboardingForm (the risky component) sits as a JSX child of &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt; inside App, rather than the error-throwing logic being inlined into App itself:
&lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
  &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;
&lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
Q: What kinds of errors can an Error Boundary NOT catch automatically?
A: Universally, React’s built-in error catching only covers synchronous errors thrown during rendering — anything outside that call stack (event handlers, useEffect callbacks, rejected promises, timers) is invisible to a boundary by default, along with errors the boundary component itself throws and errors thrown while rendering the fallback UI. In our example, an error thrown inside handleSubmit (an event handler) needs to be manually forwarded with useErrorBoundary()’s showBoundary(error):
const { showBoundary } = useErrorBoundary()
try {
  // risky work in an event handler
} catch (error) {
  showBoundary(error)
}
Q: What does resetErrorBoundary actually do?
A: Generically, it’s the boundary’s built-in recovery mechanism: calling it clears the caught error and re-mounts whatever the boundary wraps, as if rendering for the first time — a re-mount, not a restore, so any local state the wrapped component held is genuinely gone unless it was persisted outside the component beforehand. In our example, the fallback’s “Try again” button calls resetErrorBoundary to re-mount OnboardingForm fresh:
function ErrorFallback({ error, resetErrorBoundary }: FallbackProps) {
  return &lt;button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}&gt;Try again&lt;/button&gt;
}
Composition

Lesson

import { ErrorBoundary, type FallbackProps } from &#039;react-error-boundary&#039;

function ErrorFallback({ error }: FallbackProps) {
  return &lt;div role=&quot;alert&quot;&gt;Something went wrong: {error.message}&lt;/div&gt;
}

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
      &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;
    &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
  )
}

💬 The component that might throw (OnboardingForm, renamed from App) has to be a child of the ErrorBoundary, not fused into the same component — if the error happens while React is still in the process of creating the App element tree, the ErrorBoundary hasn’t been “seen” by React yet, so it can’t catch anything. Splitting into an outer App (owns the ErrorBoundary) and an inner form component is what makes the boundary effective. This mirrors try/catch: an Error Boundary only catches errors from components it wraps, and you can nest multiple boundaries for more localized error UI (e.g. separate boundaries around a list and a detail view, so an error in one doesn’t take down the other).

Other Errors

Lesson

import { useErrorBoundary } from &#039;react-error-boundary&#039;

function OnboardingForm() {
  const { showBoundary } = useErrorBoundary()

  function handleSubmit(formData: FormData) {
    try {
      // ... something that might throw
    } catch (error) {
      showBoundary(error)
    }
  }
  // ...
}

💬 React’s built-in error catching only covers errors thrown during rendering. Errors thrown from event handlers, useEffect callbacks, or a rejected promise happen outside React’s own call stack, so an Error Boundary can’t see them automatically — same reason a try/catch around a synchronous call can’t catch an error from an unrelated later callback. useErrorBoundary()’s showBoundary(error) is the manual bridge: catch the error yourself (try/catch, or a promise’s second .then argument / .catch), then hand it to the nearest Error Boundary explicitly.


💬 The complete list of what an Error Boundary can’t catch on its own: event handlers and async code (just covered above), errors thrown by the boundary component itself (a boundary can’t catch its own errors — only a boundary further up the tree can), and errors thrown while rendering the fallback UI (that also needs a boundary above it, or it takes down the tree the same as any other uncaught error). React 19 also added root-level onCaughtError/onUncaughtError options (passed to createRoot) for centralized error logging that runs alongside whatever Error Boundaries you have in place — useful for reporting even the errors a boundary does catch, since a caught error still shouldn’t go unlogged.

Reset

Lesson

function ErrorFallback({ error, resetErrorBoundary }: FallbackProps) {
  return (
    &lt;div role=&quot;alert&quot;&gt;
      Something went wrong: {error.message}
      &lt;button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}&gt;Try again&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

💬 resetErrorBoundary (passed to the fallback component alongside error) clears the error and re-mounts whatever the boundary wraps — as if it were rendering for the first time. Note “re-mount,” not “restore”: any local state the wrapped component held (typed-in form values, etc.) is genuinely gone, not preserved. If preserving that state matters, it has to be persisted somewhere outside the component (lifted state, local storage) before the reset happens.

Forms

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

React doesn’t add much on top of what forms already do natively — most of this exercise is really about the web platform’s form APIs (onSubmit, FormData, action, method, encType), plus React’s own convenience layer on top (a function-valued action prop).
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why default to method=&quot;POST&quot; instead of leaving a form on the default GET?
A: This is a plain HTML/HTTP concern, true of any form on any site: with GET, submitted field values — including passwords — get serialized into the destination URL as a query string, which then lingers in browser history, server access logs, and any analytics that capture URLs. POST moves the data into the request body instead, which isn’t logged or bookmarked the same way. In our example, the onboarding form is switched from the default GET to method=&quot;POST&quot; for exactly this reason.
&lt;form action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;&lt;!-- ... --&gt;&lt;/form&gt;
Q: Why do file uploads need encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;?
A: Also a general HTML rule: the default body encoding, application/x-www-form-urlencoded, can only serialize simple text key/value pairs, so a &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt; field gets reduced to just its filename string instead of the actual binary content. multipart/form-data encodes each field as a separate part of the request body, which is what allows real file bytes to be transmitted. In our example, the onboarding form’s photo upload only works once encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot; is added alongside method=&quot;POST&quot;.
Q: What does passing a function to a form’s action prop actually do, and why is that React-specific?
A: This is a React-only capability, not standard HTML — HTML attributes can only ever hold strings, so a function-valued action requires a framework to intercept it. When you pass a function, React calls it with a ready-made FormData object, automatically prevents the default full-page-refresh submission, and resets the form’s fields afterward — replacing the manual onSubmit + event.preventDefault() + new FormData(event.currentTarget) dance every form used to need. In our example, handleOnboarding receives the submitted FormData directly with none of that boilerplate:
function handleOnboarding(formData: FormData) {
  console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
}

&lt;form action={handleOnboarding}&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/form&gt;
Q: How do useActionState and useFormStatus relate to form actions?
A: Both are React 19 hooks that build on the same function-action mechanism generally: useActionState wraps any action to track its pending/result/error state across submissions (so you don’t manage that state by hand), and useFormStatus lets a descendant of the &lt;form&gt; — like a submit button — read whether any action on that form is currently pending, without prop-drilling. This fundamentals exercise doesn’t implement either (the onboarding form here has no pending/error UI), but they’re the natural next step once you need to show submission feedback — see useFormStatus for a full example of useFormStatus in practice.
Form

Lesson

&lt;form&gt;
  &lt;label htmlFor=&quot;usernameInput&quot;&gt;Username&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;input id=&quot;usernameInput&quot; name=&quot;username&quot; type=&quot;text&quot; /&gt;
  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;

💬 Associating a &lt;label&gt; with its input (via matching htmlFor/id, or by nesting the input inside the label) isn’t cosmetic — without it, screen readers announce the input with no name at all. Explicitly setting type=&quot;submit&quot; (or type=&quot;button&quot;) on every button is worth the extra prop: an un-typed &lt;button&gt; defaults to submit, so a decorative button nested inside a form you didn’t expect it to interact with can accidentally submit that form.

Form Action

Lesson

&lt;form action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot;&gt;&lt;!-- ... --&gt;&lt;/form&gt;

💬 By default, a form submits to the current page’s URL. The action attribute (a genuine HTML feature, not React-specific) controls the destination. With the default GET method, submitted values get serialized into that destination’s URL as a query string.

Input Types

Lesson

&lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; name=&quot;password&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot; name=&quot;photo&quot; accept=&quot;image/*&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;color&quot; name=&quot;favoriteColor&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;date&quot; name=&quot;startDate&quot; /&gt;

💬 The full list of standard input types covers a lot of built-in behavior for free (password masks input and is excluded from back/forward-cache restoration for security; file + accept restricts the file picker). Native inputs like color and date cover MVP needs but are hard to restyle — for a fully custom look, reach for a library like Radix UI or React Aria Components.

Submission

Lesson

&lt;form
  action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot;
  method=&quot;POST&quot;
  encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;
  onSubmit={(event) =&gt; {
    event.preventDefault()
    const formData = new FormData(event.currentTarget)
    console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
  }}
&gt;
  {/* ... */}
&lt;/form&gt;

💬 Three separate problems, three separate fixes: (1) GET puts every field — including passwords — into the URL, which ends up in browser history and server logs, so switch to method=&quot;POST&quot; to move data into the request body; (2) the default body encoding (application/x-www-form-urlencoded) can only serialize a file as its filename string, so encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot; is required for real file uploads; (3) neither of those stops the browser’s full-page refresh on submit — only event.preventDefault() inside onSubmit does that. event.currentTarget gives you the &lt;form&gt; DOM node, which the FormData constructor reads directly.

Form Actions

Lesson

function handleOnboarding(formData: FormData) {
  console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
}

&lt;form action={handleOnboarding}&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/form&gt;

💬 The action prop accepting a function is a React-specific feature — HTML attributes can’t hold function values. Passing a function here replaces the onSubmit + preventDefault + manual FormData construction from Submission entirely: React calls the function with a ready-made FormData object, handles preventing the default browser behavior, and resets the form’s fields back to their defaults after the function runs.


💬 This exercise covers the foundation, but a real onboarding form usually also needs to show a pending state while the action runs and surface a validation error afterward. React 19 builds two hooks directly on top of this same action mechanism for exactly that: useActionState wraps an action to track its pending/result/error state across submissions, and useFormStatus lets a descendant of the &lt;form&gt; (like a submit button) read whether any action on that form is currently pending, without prop-drilling it down. Out of scope for this fundamentals workshop, but the natural next step once you’re building real forms.

Inputs

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Form elements mostly work the same in React as in HTML, with a handful of React-specific exceptions — especially around default values, which this exercise walks through one input type at a time.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What’s the difference between value and defaultValue on an input?
A: This is React’s general controlled-vs-uncontrolled distinction, applicable to every input type: setting value makes the input “controlled” — React pins it to exactly that value on every render, so the user can’t type into it at all unless you also wire up onChange to update that value yourself. defaultValue (a React-only prop, not in the HTML spec) sets only the initial value, after which the DOM manages the input’s state and the user can freely edit it — the right choice whenever you want “pre-fill but stay editable.” In our example, every input in the “Default Value” section uses defaultValue/defaultChecked (never value) precisely so users can still type over the pre-filled data:
&lt;input type=&quot;number&quot; defaultValue={18} /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; defaultChecked /&gt;
Q: How does an unchecked checkbox or empty radio group show up in submitted FormData?
A: This is standard web-platform behavior for any HTML form, not something React introduces: an unchecked checkbox or a radio group with nothing selected simply has its field absent from FormData entirely — never false, never an empty string, just missing — while a checked checkbox submits the literal string &quot;on&quot; unless you give it an explicit value. In our example, the waiverSigned checkbox and the visibility radio group both follow this rule, so checking for the field’s presence (not its truthiness) is how you’d read them server-side.
Q: Why can’t you set a value/defaultValue on a file input?
A: Browsers block this universally, in every framework, as a security measure — no code should be able to hand a user a pre-selected file, since that could be used to exfiltrate files the user never explicitly chose. To reset a file input back to empty, you need an imperative escape hatch: ref.current.value = &#039;&#039;, or forcing React to remount the input by changing its key. In our example, there’s no value/defaultValue at all on the file input in the “Hidden Inputs”/photo-upload cases — resetting it would require one of those two workarounds.
Q: Why does a &lt;select&gt; always have a value on submission even if the user never touches it, while a radio group doesn’t?
A: This follows directly from how each element is specified in HTML: a &lt;select&gt; always has some &lt;option&gt; selected — whichever one appears first by default — so it always contributes a value to FormData, whereas a radio group has no such forced default and is simply absent if nothing’s checked. In our example, that’s why the accountType select lists an explicit &lt;option value=&quot;&quot;&gt;Please select an option&lt;/option&gt; first — without it, &quot;admin&quot; (the next option) would be silently pre-selected and submitted even if the user never opened the dropdown.
&lt;select name=&quot;accountType&quot;&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;&quot;&gt;Please select an option&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;admin&quot;&gt;Admin&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;
Checkbox

Lesson

&lt;label&gt;
  &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; name=&quot;waiverSigned&quot; /&gt; I have signed the waiver
&lt;/label&gt;

💬 Checkboxes are commonly nested inside their label (rather than using htmlFor/id) — clicking the label text still toggles the box. The quirky part is how a checkbox is represented in submitted FormData: checked → the field appears with the literal string value &quot;on&quot;; unchecked → the field is absent entirely (not false, not present at all). This is a long-standing web-platform behavior, not something React introduces.

Select

Lesson

&lt;select id=&quot;accountType&quot; name=&quot;accountType&quot;&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;&quot;&gt;Please select an option&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;admin&quot;&gt;Admin&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;teacher&quot;&gt;Teacher&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;parent&quot;&gt;Parent&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;option value=&quot;student&quot;&gt;Student&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;

💬 &lt;select&gt; is a compound element — the label is on the &lt;select&gt; itself, but each &lt;option&gt;’s visible text is separate from its submitted value. Unlike an unset checkbox or radio group, a &lt;select&gt; always has a value on submission: whichever &lt;option&gt; appears first is selected by default, so an explicit “please choose” placeholder option needs to be first if you don’t want a real value pre-selected.

Radios

Lesson

&lt;fieldset&gt;
  &lt;legend&gt;Visibility&lt;/legend&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;visibility&quot; value=&quot;public&quot; /&gt; Public
  &lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;label&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;visibility&quot; value=&quot;private&quot; /&gt; Private
  &lt;/label&gt;
&lt;/fieldset&gt;

💬 A radio group needs two layers of labeling: &lt;fieldset&gt; + &lt;legend&gt; label the group as a whole (announced to assistive tech as “what am I choosing between”), while each individual &lt;label&gt; names one option. All inputs in the group share the same name — that’s what makes them mutually exclusive. Unlike &lt;select&gt;, a radio group has no forced default: if nothing is checked, the field is simply absent from FormData on submit (same absent-when-unset behavior as an unchecked checkbox).

Hidden Inputs

Lesson

&lt;input type=&quot;hidden&quot; name=&quot;orgId&quot; value=&quot;123&quot; /&gt;

💬 A hidden input submits a value with the form without the user seeing or interacting with it (invisible on screen, and skipped by screen readers). Useful for data the client already knows and doesn’t need to ask the user for — an ID, an org context, a CSRF-style token. It can also carry a JSON string (JSON.stringify({...})) if a single field needs to represent a more complex value.

Default Value

Lesson

&lt;select defaultValue=&quot;student&quot;&gt;...&lt;/select&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;number&quot; defaultValue={18} /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;color&quot; defaultValue=&quot;#002E5D&quot; /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; defaultChecked /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;radio&quot; name=&quot;visibility&quot; value=&quot;public&quot; defaultChecked /&gt;
&lt;input type=&quot;date&quot; defaultValue={new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10)} /&gt;

💬 This is the sharpest React-specific gotcha in forms: setting the value prop on an input makes it a “controlled” input — React will keep it pinned to exactly that value and the user cannot type into it unless you also wire up an onChange to update that value yourself. defaultValue (a React-only prop, not in the HTML spec) sets the initial value only, after which the user can freely change it — which is what you want for “pre-fill but stay editable” fields. Checkboxes and radios use the parallel defaultChecked prop instead of defaultValue. Everything gets coerced to a string, so defaultValue={18} and defaultValue=&quot;18&quot; are equivalent; a date’s defaultValue string must be YYYY-MM-DD to match the format the input actually submits.


💬 One input type is the exception to all of this: &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt; has no value or defaultValue at all — browsers block setting one for security (you can’t hand the user a fake pre-selected file). So “resetting” a file input to empty can’t be done via props; it takes either an imperative ref.current.value = &#039;&#039;, or the key-reset trick covered later in this workshop (changing the input’s key forces React to remount it with a clean slate).

React Fundamentals — Quiz Log
A self-quiz through the React Fundamentals workshop: one question per exercise, sourced via the epicshop MCP server’s get_quiz_instructions tool (which pulls each exercise’s real problem/solution content), then answered here as a self-check rather than back-and-forth.
1. Hello World in JS
Q: You run this code and the page stays blank — no error either:
const rootElement = document.getElementById(&#039;root&#039;)
const element = document.createElement(&#039;div&#039;)
element.textContent = &#039;Hello World&#039;
Why doesn’t “Hello World” appear, and what’s the missing line?
A: document.createElement only builds the node in memory — it’s never attached to the visible document tree. Creating and mutating an element (setting textContent) doesn’t put it on the page; only appending it to something already in the document does. Fix:
const rootElement = document.getElementById(&#039;root&#039;)
const element = document.createElement(&#039;div&#039;)
element.textContent = &#039;Hello World&#039;
rootElement.append(element) // ← the missing line
2. Raw React APIs
Q: What does createElement(&#039;div&#039;, { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }) actually return, and at what point does React touch the real DOM?
A: It returns a plain JavaScript object — a UI descriptor, not a DOM node:
const element = createElement(&#039;div&#039;, { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
console.log(element)
// { type: &#039;div&#039;, props: { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; }, ... }

createRoot(rootElement).render(element) // ← only NOW does React touch the real DOM
Nothing before that render call creates or modifies a single DOM node — createElement just builds a description of what should exist.
3. Using JSX
Q: Why does the browser throw Unexpected token &#039;&lt;&#039; when it hits raw JSX like &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;Hello World&lt;/div&gt; in a &lt;script&gt; tag, and what two things fix it?
A: Browsers only understand JavaScript, and JSX isn’t JavaScript — it’s HTML-like syntax the browser’s parser doesn’t recognize. It needs a compiler (Babel) to transform it into createElement calls first:
&lt;script src=&quot;/babel-standalone.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;text/babel&quot; data-type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
  import * as React from &#039;/react.js&#039;
  const element = &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;Hello World&lt;/div&gt;
  // Babel compiles this to: React.createElement(&#039;div&#039;, { className: &#039;container&#039;, children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
&lt;/script&gt;
Two things fix it: (1) type=&quot;text/babel&quot; tells Babel to transpile this script instead of the browser trying to parse it directly, and (2) import * as React puts React in scope, since the compiled output calls React.createElement(...).
4. Custom Components
Q: Given Kent’s definition — “components are functions which accept an object called props and return something renderable” — why does &lt;message&gt;Hello World&lt;/message&gt; (lowercase) fail to render your message function, while &lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt; (capitalized) works?
A: Babel decides how to compile a JSX tag purely from its casing:
function message(props) {
  return &lt;div className=&quot;message&quot;&gt;{props.children}&lt;/div&gt;
}

&lt;message&gt;Hello World&lt;/message&gt;
// compiles to: createElement(&#039;message&#039;, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
// → React looks for a native DOM element called &quot;message&quot; — doesn&#039;t exist, warns/fails

&lt;Message&gt;Hello World&lt;/Message&gt;
// compiles to: createElement(Message, { children: &#039;Hello World&#039; })
// → React calls the Message function itself
A lowercase tag name compiles to a string, which React interprets as a request for a native DOM element. A capitalized tag compiles to a reference, passing the actual function so React can call it. Capitalizing components is the convention that makes this distinction visible in the code.
5. TypeScript with React
Q: Typing operator: string on CalculatorProps technically works, but why is it a bad type — and what one keyword lets operations stay the single source of truth for which operators are valid, without you having to widen or hand-list them elsewhere?
A: string accepts any string, including &#039;**&#039; — which compiles fine but throws at runtime because operations[&#039;**&#039;] doesn’t exist:
type CalculatorProps = { left: number; operator: string; right: number }
// ✅ compiles, 💥 crashes at runtime:
&lt;Calculator left={2} operator=&quot;**&quot; right={3} /&gt;
The fix is deriving the type from the operations object itself instead of hand-writing (and duplicating) a union, using satisfies so the object’s own narrow literal keys aren’t widened away:
type OperationFn = (left: number, right: number) =&gt; number

const operations = {
  &#039;+&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left + right,
  &#039;-&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left - right,
  &#039;*&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left * right,
  &#039;/&#039;: (left: number, right: number) =&gt; left / right,
} satisfies Record&lt;string, OperationFn&gt;

type Operator = keyof typeof operations // &#039;+&#039; | &#039;-&#039; | &#039;*&#039; | &#039;/&#039;, derived, not duplicated
type CalculatorProps = { left: number; operator: Operator; right: number }
6. Styling
Q: In a Box component that merges a default style with a caller-provided one —
style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}
— why does the caller’s style prop win over fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, and how would you flip that so fontStyle can never be overridden?
A: Object spread follows plain JS rules: whatever comes later in the object literal wins on key collisions.
// caller&#039;s fontStyle can override the default — spread comes last:
style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}

// component&#039;s fontStyle always wins — spread comes first:
style={{ ...style, fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039; }}
Spreading ...style after fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039; lets any fontStyle the caller passes override the default. Flipping the order to { ...style, fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039; } makes the component’s value always win — the caller can only fill in whatever else style doesn’t already define.
7. Forms
Q: A form submission is losing full page state (annoying), leaking a password into the URL (dangerous), and uploading a file as just its filename string (broken). Three separate problems — what three separate attributes/handlers fix each one, and why doesn’t fixing one also fix the others?
A: They’re independent because each addresses a different default browser behavior:
&lt;form
  action=&quot;api/onboarding&quot;
  method=&quot;POST&quot;                      // (1) fields go in the request body, not the URL
  encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot;      // (2) lets real file bytes be sent, not just a filename
  onSubmit={(event) =&gt; {
    event.preventDefault()           // (3) stops the browser&#039;s default full-page refresh
    const formData = new FormData(event.currentTarget)
    console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
  }}
&gt;
  {/* ... */}
&lt;/form&gt;
(1) the default method=&quot;GET&quot; serializes all fields — including the password — into the URL, so method=&quot;POST&quot; moves the payload into the request body instead; (2) the default body encoding (application/x-www-form-urlencoded) can only represent a file as its filename string, so encType=&quot;multipart/form-data&quot; is required for real file bytes to be sent; (3) neither of those stops the browser’s default full-page refresh on submit — only event.preventDefault() does that. None of the three implies the others. (React’s function-valued action prop, covered later in the same exercise, replaces all three at once.)
8. Inputs
Q: A form has &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; name=&quot;waiverSigned&quot; /&gt;. What ends up in the submitted FormData when the box is checked, and what happens when it’s left unchecked? Why doesn’t setting value=&quot;true&quot; and reading it as a boolean work here?
A: Checked → the field is present with the literal string &quot;on&quot;. Unchecked → the field is absent from FormData entirely — not false, not present with an empty value, just missing.
const formData = new FormData(formElement)
console.log(Object.fromEntries(formData))
// checkbox checked:   { waiverSigned: &#039;on&#039;, /* ...other fields */ }
// checkbox unchecked:  { /* waiverSigned key is just not there at all */ }

// so read it by presence, not by coercing a value:
const waiverSigned = formData.has(&#039;waiverSigned&#039;)
This is a long-standing web-platform quirk (not React’s doing), which is why you check for the field’s presence rather than trying to coerce its value to a boolean.
9. Error Boundaries
Q: Why can’t you wrap your top-level App component’s own JSX in an &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt; if the error happens while App’s elements are being created — and what’s the structural fix the workshop uses?
A: JSX only describes elements via createElement calls; a component function isn’t actually invoked until React later processes that description. If the error is thrown while App itself is being turned into elements, React hasn’t “seen” any ErrorBoundary yet — the boundary and the failure are in the same unfinished tree.
// ❌ doesn&#039;t work — if OnboardingForm&#039;s own body throws while App renders,
// there&#039;s no boundary wrapping it yet at that point:
function App() {
  return (
    &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
      {/* ...App&#039;s own JSX that might itself throw... */}
    &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
  )
}

// ✅ works — split into two components so the risky one is a genuine child:
function OnboardingForm() {
  /* the component that might throw */
}

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={ErrorFallback}&gt;
      &lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;
    &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
  )
}
The fix: rename the original component to something like OnboardingForm, and make a new outer App that renders &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;&lt;OnboardingForm /&gt;&lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;. Now the risky component is a genuine child the boundary can catch errors from.
10. Rendering Arrays
Q: Given items.map(item =&gt; &lt;li&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;), why does React warn about a missing key, and specifically why is items.map((item, index) =&gt; &lt;li key={index}&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;) usually not a real fix?
A: When an array of elements re-renders after a change, React can’t tell how it changed — a reordered array and an array whose items all got new text look structurally identical without extra information.
// ❌ no identity information — React guesses from position:
{items.map((item) =&gt; &lt;li&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;)}

// ❌ index &quot;key&quot; is still just position — same problem once items reorder/get removed:
{items.map((item, index) =&gt; &lt;li key={index}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;)}

// ✅ a real, stable identity — React can match old elements to new by data, not position:
{items.map((item) =&gt; &lt;li key={item.id}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;)}
The key supplies the missing identity, so React can match old elements to new ones by the data they represent instead of guessing from position. Using the array index as the key just re-encodes “position” as the key — behaviorally identical to having no key at all once the array is reordered or an item is removed from the middle, so it fails to fix exactly the bug it’s meant to solve. The key needs to come from the item’s own stable identity (a real ID).

Rendering Arrays

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Rendering a hard-coded list of elements is unambiguous to React — but interpolating an array of elements (list.map(item =&gt; &lt;li&gt;...&lt;/li&gt;)) removes information React needs to track changes over time, which is why React warns about a missing key prop whenever you render an array.
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: Why does React need a key prop when rendering arrays?
A: Generally, React’s reconciliation algorithm diffs the previous render’s element tree against the new one to decide what DOM work is actually needed, and for a list it needs some way to match “this element in the old tree” to “this element in the new tree” — key is that stable identity. Without it, React falls back to matching by position, which looks identical whether an item was added at the start or every item’s content shifted down by one — and that ambiguity breaks anything with state tied to a specific DOM node (focus, input values, text selection, animations). In our example, giving each &lt;li&gt; key={item.id} lets React correctly track each list item across re-renders instead of guessing from position.
&lt;ul&gt;
  {items.map((item) =&gt; (
    &lt;li key={item.id}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;
  ))}
&lt;/ul&gt;
Q: Why is using the array index as a key a bad idea?
A: In general, the index is position — exactly the information a key exists to replace — so whenever items are inserted, removed, or reordered, index-based keys cause React to misattribute old state (focus, inputs, component instances) to the wrong item, the same class of bug as having no key at all. In our example, this shows up as: select text in an input, delete a preceding item so the list shifts, and the selection/focus follows the position rather than the item, because the index-derived key didn’t move with the data.
Q: What are the rules for a valid key?
A: As a general rule, a key must uniquely identify a piece of data among its siblings in that one array (not globally — the same key value can be reused in an unrelated list elsewhere in the app) and must stay stable across re-renders of the same logical item. In our example, generating a key with Math.random() or Date.now() inside .map() would violate the stability rule — a fresh key every render tells React “this is a brand-new element” every time, forcing a full remount and state loss on every render.
Q: What else can changing a key be used for, besides array rendering?
A: More generally, a key isn’t only for lists — changing any element’s key, for any reason, tells React “treat this as a conceptually new element,” which makes React unmount the old instance (discarding all its internal state) and mount a fresh one. This is a recognized technique for forcibly resetting a component’s state without writing explicit “clear the fields” logic. In our example, bumping a resetKey counter and using it as the &lt;input&gt;’s key clears the input by remounting it.
const [resetKey, setResetKey] = useState(0)

&lt;input key={resetKey} /&gt;
&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setResetKey((k) =&gt; k + 1)}&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
Key prop

Lesson

&lt;ul&gt;
  {items.map((item) =&gt; (
    &lt;li key={item.id}&gt;{item.value}&lt;/li&gt;
  ))}
&lt;/ul&gt;

💬 When an array of elements re-renders after a change, React can’t tell how it changed — an item added at the start looks identical, structurally, to every item’s content shifting down by one. Without a key, React just guesses (typically: keep elements in position, update their content) — which is fine for stateless text, but breaks badly for anything with state the DOM itself is tracking (an input’s typed value, focus, text selection). The rule: every element in a rendered array needs a key prop that uniquely identifies the data it represents — an item’s real ID, not its position.

