Lesson


💬 This step is exploratory rather than a code change: it demonstrates that using the array index as a key produces the same problems as no key at all — because the index is really just “position,” which is exactly the information a key is supposed to replace. If you select text in an input, then delete a preceding item so items shift, the selection/focus follows the position, not the item, when the key is missing or is the index. Only a key derived from the item’s own stable identity (an ID) lets React correctly track “this specific piece of UI state belongs to this specific piece of data” as the array reorders.


💬 Two more rules about keys worth internalizing: they only need to be unique among siblings within that one array — reusing the same key value across two unrelated lists elsewhere in the app is completely fine, keys aren’t a global namespace. And a key must be stable across re-renders of the same logical item — generating one with Math.random() or Date.now() inside the .map() defeats the entire purpose, since a fresh key every render tells React “this is a brand-new element” every time, forcing a full remount (and state loss) on every single render instead of preventing it.

