💬 A key isn’t only for array reconciliation — changing an element’s key for any reason tells React “this is conceptually a new element,” so it unmounts the old instance (discarding all its internal state) and mounts a fresh one in its place. Deliberately changing a key is therefore a legitimate technique for forcibly resetting a component’s (or a DOM element’s) state, without writing any explicit “clear the fields” logic — not something reached for often, but useful to recognize.

Styling

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

Two primary ways to style in React: the style prop (inline) and the className prop (CSS classes) — both are property names, not the HTML attribute names (class, not a string of raw CSS).
Interview Q&amp;A
Q: How does the style prop differ between HTML and JSX?
A: This is a general JSX convention, not just a React quirk: HTML’s inline style attribute is a single CSS string, but JSX props are JS expressions, so style takes a JS object with camelCased property names mirroring the DOM’s CSSStyleDeclaration (e.g. background-color → backgroundColor), and bare numbers get px appended automatically for most properties. In our example, style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039; }} is an object literal passed through a JSX expression ({}), not special syntax.
&lt;div style={{ backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039;, marginTop: 20 }}&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;
Q: How do you let a component’s caller override its default styles/classes?
A: This is a general “component wraps a native element” pattern in React: accept className/style as props on your component, then merge them with your own defaults so the caller’s values win — spread order controls precedence, since a later key in an object literal overwrites an earlier one with the same name. In our example, Box does this with style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }} (caller’s style spread after the default, so it can override fontStyle) and className={box ${className}} for classes.
function Box({ className = &#039;&#039;, style, ...rest }: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt;) {
  return &lt;div className={`box ${className}`} style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }} {...rest} /&gt;
}
Q: What’s the benefit of a typed size prop over exposing raw class names?
A: More generally, this is an API-design principle: hide implementation details (like specific CSS class names) behind a small, typed surface so callers can’t couple to internals that might change, and so they get editor autocomplete instead of having to memorize magic strings. In our example, Box accepts size?: &#039;small&#039; | &#039;medium&#039; | &#039;large&#039; and internally maps it to box--${size}, keeping that class-naming scheme private to the component.
Q: What problem does a library like clsx solve?
A: In general, conditionally combining CSS class names by hand ([cond &amp;&amp; &#039;class&#039;, ...].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;)) is a common but easy-to-get-wrong pattern once you have more than a couple of conditions — clsx (or its predecessor classnames) standardizes it, including an object syntax for toggling classes by boolean flags. In our example, Box’s [box, sizeClassName, className].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;) is exactly the pattern clsx(&#039;box&#039;, size &amp;&amp; box—${size}, className) would replace.
Styling

Lesson

&lt;div className=&quot;box box--small&quot; style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039; }}&gt;
  small lightblue box
&lt;/div&gt;

💬 In HTML, inline style is a single string of CSS; in JSX it’s an object, with camelCased property names (matching the DOM’s CSSStyleDeclaration, e.g. background-color → backgroundColor). The style={{ ... }} double-curly isn’t special syntax — it’s just a JSX expression ({}) containing an object literal ({}). Numeric values (e.g. marginTop: 20) get px appended automatically.

Custom Component

Lesson

function Box({ className = &#039;&#039;, style, ...rest }: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt;) {
  return (
    &lt;div
      className={`box ${className}`}
      style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}
      {...rest}
    /&gt;
  )
}

💬 React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt; borrows the full prop type (all valid HTML attributes) for a given intrinsic element — the standard way to type a component that wraps and forwards props to a native element. Plucking off className and style lets Box merge its own defaults with whatever the caller passes, while ...rest (including children) is spread onto the underlying div untouched. Placement controls precedence: spreading style after fontStyle lets the caller override it; putting fontStyle after the spread would make it non-overridable.

Size Prop

Lesson

function Box({
  className = &#039;&#039;,
  style,
  size,
  ...rest
}: React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt; &amp; { size?: &#039;small&#039; | &#039;medium&#039; | &#039;large&#039; }) {
  const sizeClassName = size ? `box--${size}` : &#039;&#039;
  return (
    &lt;div
      className={[`box`, sizeClassName, className].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;)}
      style={{ fontStyle: &#039;italic&#039;, ...style }}
      {...rest}
    /&gt;
  )
}

// &lt;Box size=&quot;small&quot; style={{ backgroundColor: &#039;lightblue&#039; }}&gt;small lightblue box&lt;/Box&gt;

💬 Rather than making callers know which raw class name produces which visual effect, the component exposes a typed size prop and translates it internally — the styling implementation (box--small etc.) becomes a private detail of Box, and callers get autocomplete instead of having to memorize class name strings. React.ComponentProps&lt;&#039;div&#039;&gt; &amp; { size?: ... } is a TypeScript intersection — combining “all div props” with one custom prop.


💬 The [...].filter(Boolean).join(&#039; &#039;) pattern above is exactly what a tiny, ubiquitous utility library — clsx (or its predecessor classnames) — exists to replace: clsx(&#039;box&#039;, size &amp;&amp; box—${size}, className). It also handles object syntax for toggling classes by condition (clsx({ &#039;is-active&#039;: isActive })). Worth reaching for as soon as conditional class logic grows past two or three classes, rather than reinventing this join pattern in every component.

Using JSX

Notes from the EpicReact “React Fundamentals” workshop by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.

JSX is HTML-like syntactic sugar that compiles to createElement calls. Because browsers can’t evaluate JSX natively, it needs a compiler (Babel, or a build tool in real apps) to convert it first.
const element = &lt;h1 id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;Hey there&lt;/h1&gt;

// ↓ compiles to ↓

const element = createElement(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; })

💬 The single most useful skill for getting fluent with JSX: train your brain to see the compiled createElement version whenever you look at JSX, and vice versa.

The Modern (Automatic) JSX Runtime

💬 This workshop uses Babel’s classic transform, which is why every example compiles to React.createElement(...) and needs React in scope. Modern tooling (Vite, Next.js, and current Babel/TypeScript defaults) uses the newer automatic runtime instead: JSX compiles to calls like _jsx(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; }), importing jsx/jsxs automatically from react/jsx-runtime at the top of the file — code you never write yourself. That’s the real reason modern React files don’t need import React from &#039;react&#039; just to use JSX anymore; it’s not that JSX stopped needing a runtime, it’s that the import is now injected by the compiler instead of written by hand.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What is JSX, and why do browsers need help running it?
A: In general, JSX is HTML-like syntactic sugar for nested function calls that describe UI — it isn’t valid JavaScript on its own, so a compiler (Babel, SWC, TypeScript, or whatever a bundler uses) must transform it into plain JS calls before a browser (or Node) can run it. In our example, &lt;h1 id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;Hey there&lt;/h1&gt; compiles to createElement(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; }).
const element = &lt;h1 id=&quot;greeting&quot;&gt;Hey there&lt;/h1&gt;
// compiles to: createElement(&#039;h1&#039;, { id: &#039;greeting&#039;, children: &#039;Hey there&#039; })
Q: Why don’t modern React files need import React from &#039;react&#039; just to use JSX?
A: Generally, this changed because of a compiler-level shift: the older classic JSX transform compiled tags to React.createElement(...), requiring React to be in scope, whereas the newer automatic runtime (default in modern Vite/Next.js/Babel/TS configs) compiles to calls like _jsx(...) and auto-injects the needed jsx/jsxs import from react/jsx-runtime — code you never write by hand. In our example/this workshop specifically, the classic transform is used on purpose (to keep createElement visible), which is why import * as React is still required there.
Q: What can go inside JSX curly braces {}?
A: As a general JS rule carried into JSX, {} is a doorway into JavaScript, but only for expressions — things that evaluate to a value (a variable, a ternary, an IIFE) — never statements (if, for, let x = 1), since JSX curlies compile to a single expression’s worth of function arguments. This applies identically in children position and in prop/attribute position. In our example, {className} and {children} interpolate variables, and a conditional render would use a ternary or &amp;&amp;, not a bare if.
const className = &#039;container&#039;
const element = &lt;div className={className}&gt;{children}&lt;/div&gt;
Q: How does prop spreading resolve conflicts in JSX?
A: Generally, JSX spread behaves exactly like object spread (Object.assign semantics) — whichever prop appears later in source order wins a conflict, whether that’s a spread object or an explicit prop. In our example, &lt;div {...props} className=&quot;override-wins&quot; /&gt; lets the explicit className win over whatever was in props; reversing the order (&lt;div className=&quot;x&quot; {...props} /&gt;) would let the spread win instead — including for children.
Q: Why does {count &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;} sometimes render a stray 0?
A: This is a general JS/React gotcha, not specific to any one component: React treats false, null, and undefined as “render nothing,” but 0 and NaN are still valid, visible children in JS’s &amp;&amp; short-circuit — so when the left-hand value is falsy-but-numeric (0), it gets printed literally instead of hiding the badge. In our example, {count &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;} prints a stray 0 when count is 0; the fix is forcing a real boolean: {count &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;}.
Q: Why do Fragments (&lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;) exist?
A: More generally, a single JavaScript expression can only evaluate to one value, and createElement(...)/JSX ultimately has to return one thing — so returning multiple sibling elements from a component needs some wrapping container, and Fragments provide one that adds zero extra DOM nodes. In our example, wrapping a &lt;p&gt; and a &lt;ul&gt; in &lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt; groups them as one React element without adding a wrapper div that could break CSS Grid/Flexbox/table layouts relying on direct child structure.
const element = (
  &lt;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Here&#039;s Sam&#039;s favorite food:&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;ul className=&quot;sams-food&quot;&gt;...&lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/&gt;
)
