Lesson

To run JSX in the browser without a build step, load Babel Standalone and mark the script for it to compile:
&lt;script src=&quot;/babel-standalone.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script type=&quot;text/babel&quot; data-type=&quot;module&quot;&gt;
  import * as React from &#039;/react.js&#039;
  const element = &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;Hello World&lt;/div&gt;
  // ...
&lt;/script&gt;

💬 type=&quot;text/babel&quot; tells Babel to transpile the script (the browser ignores it otherwise, since it isn’t a real type it understands); data-type=&quot;module&quot; tells Babel to re-emit the compiled output as an ES module so import keeps working. import * as React (rather than import { createElement }) matters because the compiler rewrites JSX to React.createElement(...), so React needs to be in scope.

Interpolation

Lesson

const className = &#039;container&#039;
const children = &#039;Hello World&#039;
const element = &lt;div className={className}&gt;{children}&lt;/div&gt;

💬 Curly braces {} are a doorway from “JSX land” into “JavaScript land” — anything inside must be an expression (something that evaluates to a value), never a statement. You can’t put an if or a for loop directly inside {}, but a ternary or an immediately-invoked arrow function works, because those are expressions. This works the same in the children position and in attribute/prop position.

Spread props

Lesson

const props = { children: &#039;Hello World&#039;, className: &#039;container&#039; }
const element = &lt;div {...props} className=&quot;override-wins&quot; /&gt;

💬 JSX prop spreading behaves exactly like object spread (Object.assign under the hood) — whatever comes later wins in a conflict. So &lt;div {...props} className=&quot;x&quot; /&gt; lets className=&quot;x&quot; override props.className, while &lt;div className=&quot;x&quot; {...props} /&gt; lets props.className win instead. The same rule applies to children: text between the opening/closing tags overrides a children prop set earlier in the spread.

Nesting JSX

Lesson

const element = (
  &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Here&#039;s Sam&#039;s favorite food:&lt;/p&gt;
