💬 This is the direct payoff of JSX over raw createElement — you can paste in near-verbatim HTML. The one gotcha: JSX props map to DOM properties, not HTML attributes (class → className, for → htmlFor, tabindex → tabIndex). React warns in the console whenever you use the attribute name instead of the property name.


💬 A related gotcha for conditional rendering: React renders false, null, and undefined as nothing at all, but 0 and NaN still render (they’re valid, visible children). So {count &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;} silently prints a stray 0 on the page when count is 0 — a very common real-world bug. The idiomatic fix is to make the condition explicitly boolean: {count &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; &lt;Badge /&gt;}.

Fragments

Lesson

const element = (
  &lt;&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Here&#039;s Sam&#039;s favorite food:&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;ul className=&quot;sams-food&quot;&gt;...&lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/&gt;
)

💬 &lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt; (shorthand for &lt;React.Fragment&gt;...&lt;/React.Fragment&gt;) groups sibling elements without adding a wrapper DOM node. It exists because a single JavaScript expression can’t evaluate to two separate values — createElement(...), createElement(...) isn’t valid, so you need a container element without an actual DOM representation. Useful anywhere the DOM structure matters for CSS (Grid, Flexbox, tables) and an extra wrapper div would break the layout.

useState vs useReducer

Article by Kent C. Dodds. Notes and clarifications by me are marked with &gt; 💬.


Interview Q&amp;A
Q: When should you use useState vs useReducer?
A: useState and useReducer are both just state-management primitives — useState is actually implemented as a thin wrapper over useReducer internally, so it’s a spectrum, not two unrelated tools. The general rule: reach for useState for state that’s simple and independent (a boolean, a string, an isolated value), and reach for useReducer once you have several related values that change together, a fixed set of named transitions (a state machine, e.g. idle → loading → success | error), or update logic complex/branchy enough that you want it out of the component body and independently testable. In our example, useDarkMode is one independent value → useState wins; useUndo has past/present/future that must always move together → useReducer wins.
// independent value → useState
const [mode, setMode] = useState(&#039;light&#039;)

// several values that change together, named transitions → useReducer
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(undoReducer, { past: [], present, future: [] })
dispatch({ type: &#039;UNDO&#039; })
Q: Is useReducer strictly more powerful than useState?
A: Yes, structurally — useState(init) behaves the same as useReducer with a reducer that just returns whatever new value it’s given, which is exactly how React implements useState under the hood. That means anything expressible with useState can be rewritten with useReducer, but not everything reducer logic can naturally express (multiple coordinated fields, action-based transitions) is comfortable to hand-roll with several separate useState calls. In our example, Version C of useDarkMode deliberately reimplements useState’s exact behavior with useReducer to prove the point — and it’s strictly worse for that use case, since simplifying a reducer this far just reinvents useState with more ceremony.
// useState(init) is roughly:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer((_, newValue) =&gt; newValue, init)
const setState = (v) =&gt; dispatch(typeof v === &#039;function&#039; ? v(state) : v)
Q: What bug can appear when related state is split across multiple useState calls, and how does useReducer avoid it?
A: This is the classic stale closure problem: any callback (an event handler, an effect, a memoized function) captures the values of state variables from the render it was created in, so if two updates to related state fire within the same render cycle — before a re-render lets the closures refresh — the second one can read and act on outdated values from the first, silently clobbering it. useReducer sidesteps this by design: the reducer always receives the current, just-committed state as an argument from React itself, not from a closure, so there’s nothing to go stale. (A callback-form setState(prev =&gt; next) fixes the same bug for useState, but you have to remember to do it every time; a reducer makes it the only option.) In our example, useUndo’s buggy useState version loses an entry when two useEffects each call set() in the same render pass — both read present: &#039;first&#039; from their own stale closures — while the useReducer version is immune because dispatch never reads state itself.
// stale-closure-safe update, either approach:
setState((prevState) =&gt; ({ ...prevState, present: newPresent })) // useState, callback form
dispatch({ type: &#039;SET&#039;, newPresent }) // useReducer — always safe by construction
Q: Why is dispatch from useReducer useful for passing updates through context?
A: Both useState’s setter and useReducer’s dispatch are guaranteed by React to keep the same function identity across renders — but dispatch is often preferred for context because it’s a single, generic entry point (“send an action”) rather than many distinct setters, which makes it easy to memoize and pass down as one stable value. Since it never changes reference, a component that only calls dispatch (and doesn’t read the state itself) can be wrapped in React.memo and will skip re-rendering when the state updates — only components that actually subscribe to the state value re-render. In our example, the note’s “Quick decision guide” calls this out explicitly as a useReducer-favoring case: “Passing updater down through context → useReducer (stable dispatch).”
const DispatchContext = createContext(null)
// A component that only calls dispatch(...) via this context never
// re-renders when state changes, since dispatch&#039;s identity never changes.
Q: Are reducers easier to test than useState-based logic?
A: Generally yes — a reducer is a pure function (state, action) =&gt; newState with no hooks, no React runtime, and no DOM involved, so every state transition can be exercised with a plain function call and a plain assertion, independent of rendering anything. Logic spread across several useCallbacks closing over useState values is coupled to React’s render cycle, so it typically has to be tested by rendering the component/hook (e.g. with @testing-library/react-hooks) and simulating interactions. In our example, undoReducer can be tested directly as undoReducer(state, { type: &#039;UNDO&#039; }) with no component involved at all, whereas the equivalent useUndo useState version needs a full render to verify.
expect(undoReducer(state, { type: &#039;UNDO&#039; })).toEqual(expectedState)

For a deep dive on why the useUndo useState bug happens and the broader re-rendering model it illustrates, see React Re-rendering.

Framing the Question
Whenever two things seem to do the same thing, people ask: “When do I use one over the other?” There are usually two reasons multiple approaches exist:

One is outdated (e.g. class components vs function components — class is the old way).
Each involves trade-offs suited to different scenarios.

useState and useReducer are case 2. Neither is better — they are tuned for different situations.

The Two Rules
Kent’s answer boils down to two sentences:

“When it’s just an independent element of state you’re managing: useState”
“When one element of your state relies on the value of another element of your state in order to update: useReducer”


💬 “Relies on the value of another element” means: to compute the next value of state A, you need to read the current value of state B. When that’s the case, having them in separate useState calls creates a closure-bug risk — see the useUndo section below.


Example 1 — useDarkMode
This example shows a case where useState is the right choice.
Version A: with useState ✅
function useDarkMode() {
  const preferDarkQuery = &#039;(prefers-color-scheme: dark)&#039;
  const [mode, setMode] = React.useState(
    () =&gt;
      window.localStorage.getItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;) ||
      (window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery).matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;),
  )

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const mediaQuery = window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery)
    const handleChange = () =&gt; setMode(mediaQuery.matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;)
    mediaQuery.addListener(handleChange)
    return () =&gt; mediaQuery.removeListener(handleChange)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    window.localStorage.setItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;, mode)
  }, [mode])

  return [mode, setMode]
}

💬 The lazy initializer (the () =&gt; function passed to useState) runs only on mount — it reads localStorage and the system media query once to set the initial value. The first effect syncs back to localStorage whenever mode changes. The second effect listens to OS-level dark-mode changes (e.g. the user switches their system theme). Clean and flat.

Version B: with useReducer — verbose Redux-style ❌
const preferDarkQuery = &#039;(prefers-color-scheme: dark)&#039;

function darkModeReducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case &#039;MEDIA_CHANGE&#039;: {
      return { ...state, mode: action.mode }
    }
    case &#039;SET_MODE&#039;: {
      return { ...state, mode: action.mode }
    }
    default: {
      throw new Error(`Unhandled action type: ${action.type}`)
    }
  }
}

function init() {
  return {
    mode:
      window.localStorage.getItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;) ||
      (window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery).matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;),
  }
}

function useDarkMode() {
  const [state, dispatch] = React.useReducer(
    darkModeReducer,
    { mode: &#039;light&#039; },
    init,
  )
  const { mode } = state

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const mediaQuery = window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery)
    const handleChange = () =&gt;
      dispatch({
        type: &#039;MEDIA_CHANGE&#039;,
        mode: mediaQuery.matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;,
      })
    mediaQuery.addListener(handleChange)
    return () =&gt; mediaQuery.removeListener(handleChange)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    window.localStorage.setItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;, mode)
  }, [mode])

  const setMode = React.useCallback(
    (newMode) =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;SET_MODE&#039;, mode: newMode }),
    [],
  )

  return [mode, setMode]
}

💬 useReducer takes three arguments: (reducer, initialArg, init). When you pass init, React calls init(initialArg) to compute the real initial state — equivalent to useState’s lazy initializer. This pattern is useful when you want to reset state back to its initial value later (just dispatch and call init again inside the reducer). Here it’s just added ceremony.

Version C: with useReducer — simplified, but still worse ❌
function useDarkMode() {
  const preferDarkQuery = &#039;(prefers-color-scheme: dark)&#039;
  const [mode, setMode] = React.useReducer(
    (prevMode, nextMode) =&gt;
      typeof nextMode === &#039;function&#039; ? nextMode(prevMode) : nextMode,
    &#039;light&#039;,
    () =&gt;
      window.localStorage.getItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;) ||
      (window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery).matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;),
  )

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const mediaQuery = window.matchMedia(preferDarkQuery)
    const handleChange = () =&gt; setMode(mediaQuery.matches ? &#039;dark&#039; : &#039;light&#039;)
    mediaQuery.addListener(handleChange)
    return () =&gt; mediaQuery.removeListener(handleChange)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    window.localStorage.setItem(&#039;colorMode&#039;, mode)
  }, [mode])

  return [mode, setMode]
}

💬 This reducer mimics useState exactly: if you dispatch a function, call it with previous state; otherwise replace state with the new value. This is literally how useState works internally. Kent’s point: if you’ve simplified useReducer this much, you’ve just reinvented useState — and the original useState version is still clearer.

Verdict: mode is a single independent value. No other state depends on it, and it doesn’t depend on any other state. useState wins.

Example 2 — useUndo
This example shows a case where useReducer is the right choice.
useUndo tracks a value through time with three arrays: past (history), present (current), and future (for redo).
Version A: useState — the buggy version ❌
function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [past, setPast] = React.useState([])
  const [present, setPresent] = React.useState(initialPresent)
  const [future, setFuture] = React.useState([])

  const canUndo = past.length !== 0
  const canRedo = future.length !== 0

  const undo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    if (!canUndo) return

    const previous = past[past.length - 1]
    const newPast = past.slice(0, past.length - 1)

    setPast(newPast)
    setPresent(previous)
    setFuture([present, ...future])
  }, [canUndo, future, past, present])

  const redo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    if (!canRedo) return

    const next = future[0]
    const newFuture = future.slice(1)

    setPast([...past, present])
    setPresent(next)
    setFuture(newFuture)
  }, [canRedo, future, past, present])

  const set = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; {
      if (newPresent === present) {
        return
      }
      setPast([...past, present])
      setPresent(newPresent)
      setFuture([])
    },
    [past, present],
  )

  const reset = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
    setPast([])
    setPresent(newPresent)
    setFuture([])
  }, [])

  return [
    { past, present, future },
    { set, reset, undo, redo, canUndo, canRedo },
  ]
}
The bug surfaces here:
function Example() {
  const [state, { set }] = useUndo(&#039;first&#039;)

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    set(&#039;second&#039;)
  }, [])

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    set(&#039;third&#039;)
  }, [])

  return &lt;pre&gt;{JSON.stringify(state, null, 2)}&lt;/pre&gt;
}

💬 Why this breaks: Each useCallback captures past, present, and future from the render in which it was created. When set(&#039;second&#039;) runs, it reads past = [] and present = &#039;first&#039; — correct. But set(&#039;third&#039;) runs in the same render cycle, so it still reads the same stale snapshot: past = [] and present = &#039;first&#039;. The second call overwrites, losing the &#039;second&#039; entry entirely.
If you add set to the dependency arrays of both effects to silence the lint warning, the effects now re-run whenever set changes. But set depends on past and present, which change when set runs — infinite loop. The two are chasing each other.

Version B: useState — the fixed version ⚠️
function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [state, setState] = React.useState({
    past: [],
    present: initialPresent,
    future: [],
  })

  const canUndo = state.past.length !== 0
  const canRedo = state.future.length !== 0

  const undo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    setState((currentState) =&gt; {
      const { past, present, future } = currentState

      if (past.length === 0) return currentState

      const previous = past[past.length - 1]
      const newPast = past.slice(0, past.length - 1)

      return {
        past: newPast,
        present: previous,
        future: [present, ...future],
      }
    })
  }, [])

  const redo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    setState((currentState) =&gt; {
      const { past, present, future } = currentState

      if (future.length === 0) return currentState

      const next = future[0]
      const newFuture = future.slice(1)

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: next,
        future: newFuture,
      }
    })
  }, [])

  const set = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
    setState((currentState) =&gt; {
      const { present, past } = currentState
      if (newPresent === present) return currentState

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: newPresent,
        future: [],
      }
    })
  }, [])

  const reset = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
    setState(() =&gt; ({
      past: [],
      present: newPresent,
      future: [],
    }))
  }, [])

  return [state, { set, reset, undo, redo, canUndo, canRedo }]
}

💬 How the fix works: Instead of reading past/present/future from the closure, we use the state updater callback form of setState(currentState =&gt; nextState). React guarantees that currentState is the most recent committed state — not a stale snapshot. So now both effects see the correct, up-to-date values. This also means set, undo, and redo have empty dependency arrays [] and never change reference, which also solves the infinite loop.
This works! But it required: (1) collapsing three separate useState calls into one object, (2) rewriting every updater as a callback, and (3) performing all the calculations inside the callback instead of in the closure. It’s correct but noticeably awkward.

Version C: useReducer — natural fit ✅
const UNDO = &#039;UNDO&#039;
const REDO = &#039;REDO&#039;
const SET = &#039;SET&#039;
const RESET = &#039;RESET&#039;

function undoReducer(state, action) {
  const { past, present, future } = state
  const { type, newPresent } = action

  switch (type) {
    case UNDO: {
      if (past.length === 0) return state

      const previous = past[past.length - 1]
      const newPast = past.slice(0, past.length - 1)

      return {
        past: newPast,
        present: previous,
        future: [present, ...future],
      }
    }

    case REDO: {
      if (future.length === 0) return state

      const next = future[0]
      const newFuture = future.slice(1)

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: next,
        future: newFuture,
      }
    }

    case SET: {
      if (newPresent === present) return state

      return {
        past: [...past, present],
        present: newPresent,
        future: [],
      }
    }

    case RESET: {
      return {
        past: [],
        present: newPresent,
        future: [],
      }
    }
  }
}

function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [state, dispatch] = React.useReducer(undoReducer, {
    past: [],
    present: initialPresent,
    future: [],
  })

  const canUndo = state.past.length !== 0
  const canRedo = state.future.length !== 0
  const undo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; dispatch({ type: UNDO }), [])
  const redo = React.useCallback(() =&gt; dispatch({ type: REDO }), [])
  const set = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; dispatch({ type: SET, newPresent }),
    [],
  )
  const reset = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; dispatch({ type: RESET, newPresent }),
    [],
  )

  return [state, { set, reset, undo, redo, canUndo, canRedo }]
}

💬 Notice how clean the hook body becomes. Each callback just dispatches — it never reads state at all. The reducer gets the current state as its first argument from React directly, so there’s never a stale closure. The stale-closure problem doesn’t arise because we never need to capture state in the closure in the first place.
Kent’s key observation: “We wouldn’t have even considered adding anything to our dependency array because those functions are so simple they don’t need anything. All the logic lives in our reducer. That helps us avoid the issue naturally.”

Verdict: past, present, and future always change together as a unit. They belong in one reducer.

Conclusion (Kent’s Words)

“Honestly, even the ‘rules’ are subjective because as I demonstrated, you can do everything you want with either one.”


“You can absolutely use useState in the same component or hook that’s using useReducer.”


“Separate state logically by domain. If it changes together, it’s likely better to keep together in a reducer. If something is pretty independent from other elements of state, then putting it with other elements of state is just adding unnecessary complexity.”


“In general, I suggest starting with useState, and moving to useReducer when you notice elements of state need to change together.”


My Additional Angles
Reducers are pure functions → easy to test
A reducer is just (state, action) =&gt; newState — no hooks, no DOM, no React. You can test every state transition with a plain function call:
expect(undoReducer(state, { type: &#039;UNDO&#039; })).toEqual(expectedState)
With useState logic spread across multiple useCallback closures, you can only test through a rendered component.
dispatch has a stable reference
useReducer returns [state, dispatch]. The dispatch function is stable — it never changes between renders (same guarantee as setState). This matters when passing updaters through context: components that only consume dispatch won’t re-render when state changes, because dispatch is the same reference every time.
useState is useReducer under the hood
React implements useState as a simplified useReducer internally. Conceptually:
// useState(init) is roughly:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer((_, newValue) =&gt; newValue, init)
const setState = (v) =&gt; dispatch(typeof v === &#039;function&#039; ? v(state) : v)
Everything useState can do, useReducer can do — but not vice versa. Kent’s Version C above is the proof.
Think in state machines
When your state has a fixed set of valid values and named transitions between them (idle → loading → success | error), useReducer maps to that model directly. Each case in the switch is one edge in the graph. This makes impossible states easier to prevent — you only return shapes you’ve explicitly coded.
Quick decision guide
Single value, no dependencies         → useState
Multiple values that move together    → useReducer
Next state depends on other state     → useReducer
Want to unit-test state logic alone   → useReducer
Passing updater down through context  → useReducer (stable dispatch)

React Re-rendering

A comprehensive reference on what causes re-renders, why they go wrong, how to diagnose them, and how to fix them. Companion to useState vs useReducer.


Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What actually causes a React component to re-render?
A: In React’s mental model there is exactly one root trigger — a state update (useState setter or useReducer dispatch) somewhere in the tree. Every other apparent cause (a parent re-rendering, a context value changing, an effect’s dependency changing) is a downstream cascade from some state change, not an independent trigger of its own; specifically, a component re-renders when its own state changes, when its parent re-renders (children re-render by default whether or not their props changed), or when a context value it subscribes to changes. In our example, the table under “The Only Real Trigger” lays out all four situations, and the note calls out the common misconception directly: props changing doesn’t itself cause a re-render, it’s the parent’s state change that re-renders the parent, which re-renders children as a side effect.
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0) // the one real trigger
  return &lt;Child label=&quot;static&quot; /&gt;        // re-renders anyway when Parent does
}
Q: Why does React.memo sometimes fail to prevent a re-render even though the data “didn’t change”?
A: React.memo only skips re-rendering when every prop is Object.is-equal (the same semantics as ===) to its previous value; primitives (numbers, strings, booleans) compare by value so this works fine, but objects, arrays, and functions compare by reference, and any one of those created fresh inside a component body is a brand-new reference on every render even when it’s structurally identical to the last one. This is arguably the single most common reason React.memo “doesn’t work” in practice. In our example, the config/handleClick snippets under “Primitives vs Non-Primitives” show a MemoizedChild re-rendering every time regardless of React.memo, because config and handleClick are re-created each render — fixed later in the note with useMemo/useCallback.
function Parent() {
  const config = { theme: &#039;dark&#039; } // new reference every render
  return &lt;MemoizedChild config={config} /&gt; // memo can&#039;t help — always &quot;changed&quot;
}
Q: What is a stale closure, and how does it happen in React?
A: A stale closure is a general JavaScript phenomenon — not React-specific — where a function keeps referencing the value a variable had at the moment the function was created, even after that variable’s “real” value has since changed elsewhere. It shows up in React because a function component is called again on every render, so each render gets its own fresh set of local variables; any function defined during that render (an effect callback, a useCallback-wrapped handler) permanently closes over that render’s snapshot of state, and if the effect only runs once ([] deps) or the callback’s deps don’t include that state, the closure never sees later updates. In our example, the “stale closure” section shows a setInterval inside a useEffect([]) that always logs count === 0, and the deeper useUndo case study shows the same root cause producing a real data-loss bug when two effects in the same render cycle both read a stale present value.
useEffect(() =&gt; {
  const id = setInterval(() =&gt; console.log(count), 1000) // count frozen at mount value
  return () =&gt; clearInterval(id)
}, []) // empty deps → this closure never refreshes
Q: What are the two correct ways to fix a stale-closure bug where state depends on other state?
A: Both fixes work by making sure the code reads state from somewhere other than a captured closure variable: (1) use the functional/updater form of the state setter — setState(currentState =&gt; nextState) — which React guarantees is always called with the most recently committed state regardless of what the closure captured, or (2) move the update logic into a useReducer reducer, since the reducer function receives the current state directly as an argument from React on every dispatch, so there’s no closure over state to go stale in the first place. In our example, “The two correct fixes” section applies both to the useUndo bug: Fix 1 rewrites set to use setState(currentState =&gt; ...) with empty deps, and Fix 2 replaces the three useState calls with a single useReducer, after which set just calls dispatch and never reads state at all.
// Fix 1: functional updater
setState((currentState) =&gt; ({ ...currentState, present: newPresent }))

// Fix 2: reducer — nothing captured, nothing to go stale
const set = useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;SET&#039;, newPresent }), [])
Q: What’s the difference between useMemo and useCallback, and when should you reach for either?
A: Both exist to give you a stable reference across renders so that a React.memo-wrapped child (or a dependency array elsewhere) doesn’t see a “changed” prop when the underlying value is actually the same; useCallback(fn, deps) is literally defined as useMemo(() =&gt; fn, deps), it’s just a more readable spelling for the specific case of memoizing a function. The general guidance from the React team is to reach for either only after profiling shows an actual, measurable problem — an expensive computation worth caching (useMemo) or a reference passed into a memoized child (useCallback/useMemo) — since React re-renders are cheap by default and the memoization bookkeeping itself isn’t free. In our example, the “Optimization Triad” section pairs this with React.memo: useMemo stabilizes the config object, useCallback stabilizes handleClick, and only then does wrapping the child in React.memo actually skip re-renders.
const config = useMemo(() =&gt; ({ theme: &#039;dark&#039; }), [])       // stable object
const handleClick = useCallback(() =&gt; doSomething(), [])     // stable function
The Only Real Trigger: State Changes
Every re-render in React starts with a state change. That is the one and only trigger. Everything else is a cascade from it.
The four situations where a component re-renders:





























CauseWhat happensOwn state changesuseState / useReducer dispatchParent re-rendersChild re-renders by default (unless memoized)Context value changesAny useContext consumer re-rendersHook deps changeThe hook re-runs, which may set state

Misconception: “Props changes cause re-renders.” Props don’t re-render a component. What happens is: a parent re-renders from its own state change, and children re-render as a side-effect of that. The child’s props might look exactly the same — it still re-renders unless you opt out.


Primitives vs Non-Primitives: The Reference Equality Trap
This is the single most common source of wasted re-renders and memoization failures.
How JavaScript compares values
// Primitives — compared by VALUE
42 === 42        // true
&#039;hello&#039; === &#039;hello&#039;  // true
true === true    // true

// Non-primitives — compared by REFERENCE (memory address)
{} === {}        // false — different objects in memory
[] === []        // false
(() =&gt; {}) === (() =&gt; {})  // false
Every time a component renders, code inside the function body runs from scratch. That means any object, array, or function defined inside the component body is a brand new reference each render.
Why this breaks React
React uses Object.is() (same semantics as ===) to compare old props with new props. When a new reference is created on every render, React sees a prop “change” even if the data is identical:
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0)

  // New object reference on every render
  const config = { theme: &#039;dark&#039; }

  // Even with React.memo, Child ALWAYS re-renders
  return &lt;MemoizedChild config={config} /&gt;
}
config is structurally identical across renders but it is never the same object. React.memo’s shallow comparison detects the new reference and re-renders the child anyway.
The same trap with functions
function Parent() {
  // New function reference on every render
  const handleClick = () =&gt; doSomething()

  return &lt;MemoizedChild onClick={handleClick} /&gt;
}
Same problem. Even though handleClick does exactly the same thing every render, it is a new closure each time.

Stale Closures: When React Captures the Wrong Value
A stale closure is what happens when a function captures a variable’s value at the time it was created, that variable later updates, but the function still holds the old value.
The counter example
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0)

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; {
    const interval = setInterval(() =&gt; {
      console.log(count)   // Always logs 0 — stale!
      setCount(count + 1)  // count is frozen as 0
    }, 1000)

    return () =&gt; clearInterval(interval)
  }, []) // ← empty deps: effect runs once, captures count=0 forever
}
The effect runs on mount and captures count = 0. The interval fires every second and increments by 0+1 each time — but it never sees the updated count because the closure is frozen at the initial render.
Why this happens in React specifically
React re-renders by calling your function component again. Each call creates a new set of variables. A closure created in render #1 holds render #1’s values forever. When render #2 happens, render #1’s closure doesn’t update — it cannot, because that’s how JavaScript closures work.

Case Study: The useUndo Stale Closure Bug
This is the exact bug described in useState vs useReducer — Version A: useState (buggy).
The code
function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [past, setPast] = React.useState([])
  const [present, setPresent] = React.useState(initialPresent)
  const [future, setFuture] = React.useState([])

  const set = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; {
      if (newPresent === present) return

      setPast([...past, present])  // reads past from closure
      setPresent(newPresent)
      setFuture([])
    },
    [past, present],  // set is recreated when these change
  )

  // ...
}

function Example() {
  const [state, { set }] = useUndo(&#039;first&#039;)

  React.useEffect(() =&gt; { set(&#039;second&#039;) }, [])
  React.useEffect(() =&gt; { set(&#039;third&#039;) }, [])

  return &lt;pre&gt;{JSON.stringify(state, null, 2)}&lt;/pre&gt;
}
Step-by-step walkthrough of the failure
Render #1 (mount)





















VariableValuepast[]present&#039;first&#039;future[]
set is created via useCallback and closes over past = [] and present = &#039;first&#039;. Both effects are registered with empty [] deps — they will run once after the first commit.
After commit: both effects run
React batches the two effects and runs them back-to-back before triggering another render. Both effects call the same set — the one that closed over the initial snapshot.
Effect 1 runs: set(&#039;second&#039;)
Closure sees:  past=[], present=&#039;first&#039;
Calls:         setPast([&#039;first&#039;])
               setPresent(&#039;second&#039;)
               setFuture([])

State updates are scheduled, not immediate. The closure’s values do not update in place.
Effect 2 runs immediately after: set(&#039;third&#039;)
Closure sees:  past=[], present=&#039;first&#039;  ← SAME stale snapshot
Calls:         setPast([&#039;first&#039;])        ← overwrites effect 1&#039;s update
               setPresent(&#039;third&#039;)
               setFuture([])

&#039;second&#039; is lost. The final state is { past: [&#039;first&#039;], present: &#039;third&#039;, future: [] }.
Why adding set to deps makes it worse
If you add set to the dependency arrays to silence the ESLint warning:
React.useEffect(() =&gt; { set(&#039;second&#039;) }, [set])
React.useEffect(() =&gt; { set(&#039;third&#039;) }, [set])
Now the effects re-run whenever set changes. But set depends on past and present. Calling set updates past and present, which recreates set, which re-runs both effects — infinite loop.
The two correct fixes
Fix 1 — State updater callback form (advanced useState)
Use setState(currentState =&gt; nextState). React guarantees currentState is the most recent committed state, bypassing the closure problem:
const set = React.useCallback((newPresent) =&gt; {
  setState((currentState) =&gt; {
    const { past, present } = currentState
    if (newPresent === present) return currentState

    return {
      past: [...past, present],
      present: newPresent,
      future: [],
    }
  })
}, []) // ← empty deps; set never changes reference
The closure no longer needs past or present — it receives them fresh from React on every invocation.
Fix 2 — useReducer (natural fit)
Move all the logic into a reducer. The reducer receives the current state as its first argument directly from React, so there are no closures over state at all:
function undoReducer(state, action) {
  const { past, present, future } = state
  // ...compute next state from current state...
}

function useUndo(initialPresent) {
  const [state, dispatch] = React.useReducer(undoReducer, {
    past: [],
    present: initialPresent,
    future: [],
  })

  const set = React.useCallback(
    (newPresent) =&gt; dispatch({ type: &#039;SET&#039;, newPresent }),
    [] // dispatch is always stable; no deps needed
  )
  // ...
}
Each callback just dispatches — it never reads state at all. Stale closures don’t arise because there’s nothing to close over.

React 18 Automatic Batching
Before React 18, React only batched state updates from synchronous event handlers. Async updates (setTimeout, Promises) each triggered a separate render.
// Before React 18: triggers 2 separate re-renders
setTimeout(() =&gt; {
  setCount(c =&gt; c + 1) // render 1
  setFlag(f =&gt; !f)     // render 2
}, 1000)
React 18 batches all updates within the same event loop tick — regardless of source:
// React 18: triggers 1 re-render
setTimeout(() =&gt; {
  setCount(c =&gt; c + 1) // \
  setFlag(f =&gt; !f)     //  → batched into one render
}, 1000)
This is why both useEffect calls in the useUndo bug run against the same stale snapshot — they execute in the same synchronous batch, before any state commits.
To opt out of batching for a specific update:
import { flushSync } from &#039;react-dom&#039;

flushSync(() =&gt; setCount(c =&gt; c + 1)) // commits immediately
flushSync(() =&gt; setFlag(f =&gt; !f))     // commits immediately after

Diagnosing Unnecessary Re-renders
React DevTools Profiler

Open browser DevTools → React DevTools → Profiler tab
Enable “Record why each component rendered while profiling” in Profiler settings
Click Record, interact with the UI, click Stop
Inspect the flame graph — wide red bars are slow; hover to see the render reason
Check Ranked chart for the top offenders by time

Key metrics:

















MetricWhat it meansactualDurationTime to render this component + all descendantsbaseDurationTime to render just this component in isolation
If actualDuration &gt;&gt; baseDuration, memoization isn’t working on the descendants.
Render reasons you’ll see:

“Hook state changed” — own state update
“Props changed” — a prop reference changed
“Parent component rendered” — caught up in a parent re-render; the child itself had no change

why-did-you-render library
Install @welldone-software/why-did-you-render in dev. It logs to the console whenever a component re-renders unnecessarily, showing you exactly which prop or state changed (and often proving the “change” was a new reference to an equal value).

The Optimization Triad: memo, useMemo, useCallback
Use these to escape the default “parent renders → children render” cascade. Diagnose first, optimize second.
React.memo
Wraps a component. Skips re-render if all props pass a shallow equality check (same as ===).
const MemoizedChild = React.memo(function Child({ config, onClick }) {
  // Only re-renders if config or onClick references change
})
memo fails the moment any prop is a new object, array, or function reference. The other two hooks solve that.
useMemo — stabilize a value reference
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0)

  // Without useMemo: new object every render → memo fails
  const config = React.useMemo(() =&gt; ({ theme: &#039;dark&#039; }), [])
  //                                                       ↑ deps

  return &lt;MemoizedChild config={config} /&gt;
}
config is now the same object reference across renders (as long as deps don’t change). React.memo can skip the child re-render.
Also use useMemo to avoid recomputing expensive values:
const sortedItems = React.useMemo(
  () =&gt; items.slice().sort(compareByDate),
  [items]
)
useCallback — stabilize a function reference
function Parent() {
  const handleClick = React.useCallback(() =&gt; {
    doSomething()
  }, []) // recreate only when deps change

  return &lt;MemoizedChild onClick={handleClick} /&gt;
}
useCallback(fn, deps) is exactly useMemo(() =&gt; fn, deps) — it just reads better for functions.
Decision guide
Is this computation expensive (&gt;1ms)?           → useMemo
Is this function passed to a memoized child?    → useCallback
Is this object/array passed to a memoized child? → useMemo
Is this a dep of another hook?                  → useMemo / useCallback
Otherwise                                        → skip it


React is highly optimized by default. Most components re-render in &lt;1ms. Pre-emptive memoization adds overhead (the memoization check itself) and cognitive cost. Profile first.

React Compiler (2025)
As of late 2025, the React Compiler (v1.0) automatically applies memoization where it is safe to do so. In projects using it, you rarely need to reach for useMemo or useCallback manually.

Common Anti-Patterns
1. Creating components inside render
// ❌ ComponentB is recreated (referentially new) on every ParentA render
function ParentA() {
  function ComponentB() { return &lt;div /&gt; }
  return &lt;ComponentB /&gt;
}

// ✅ Define at module level
function ComponentB() { return &lt;div /&gt; }
function ParentA() {
  return &lt;ComponentB /&gt;
}
When a component definition changes reference, React unmounts and remounts the old tree. This is far more expensive than a re-render.
2. Passing inline objects/arrays/functions to memoized children
// ❌ New object on every render — React.memo is useless
&lt;MemoizedChild style={{ color: &#039;red&#039; }} /&gt;

// ✅ Stable reference
const STYLE = { color: &#039;red&#039; }
&lt;MemoizedChild style={STYLE} /&gt;
// or
const style = React.useMemo(() =&gt; ({ color: &#039;red&#039; }), [])
&lt;MemoizedChild style={style} /&gt;
3. Over-using context
Every consumer of a context re-renders when any part of the context value changes. If one context holds both user and theme, a user update re-renders every component that only cares about theme.
Split contexts by update frequency:
// ❌ One context — everything re-renders on any change
&lt;AppContext.Provider value={{ user, theme, settings }} /&gt;

// ✅ Separate contexts
&lt;UserContext.Provider value={user}&gt;
  &lt;ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}&gt;
    &lt;SettingsContext.Provider value={settings}&gt;

Quick Reference
Trigger:       state change (everything else cascades from it)

Reference types that create new refs each render:
  - Objects:    { key: value }
  - Arrays:     [1, 2, 3]
  - Functions:  () =&gt; {}

Fix new refs:
  - Stable value  → useMemo
  - Stable fn     → useCallback
  - Skip child    → React.memo (needs stable props)

Stale closure fixes:
  - Functional setState: setState(prev =&gt; next)
  - Move logic to reducer (useReducer)
  - Add to deps array (if it doesn&#039;t cause loops)
  - useRef for values you need but don&#039;t want to track

Diagnose:
  - React DevTools Profiler → render reasons
  - why-did-you-render library

The use Hook — Reading Promises

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Suspense
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 1, “Data fetching”) plus the official
react.dev reference. Companion to JavaScript Promises (the mechanics
this note relies on) and use and createContext (which covers use
for context instead of promises). Notes and clarifications by me are
marked with &gt; 💬.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What does the use hook do?
A: It’s a general-purpose API for reading a resource during render — either
a context (an alternative to useContext, see use and createContext)
or a Promise. For promises specifically, it returns the resolved value
synchronously (no await) by throwing the pending promise internally and
letting the nearest Suspense boundary catch it, then re-running the
component once it settles — this is the core primitive any Suspense-based
data-fetching approach is built on, not something specific to one app. In
our example, use(shipPromise) is what lets ShipDetails read ship data
with a plain synchronous-looking line of code:
function ShipDetails({ shipPromise }: { shipPromise: Promise&lt;Ship&gt; }) {
  const ship = use(shipPromise) // suspends until shipPromise settles
  return &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;
}
Q: Why can’t you just await inside a component?
A: This is a general constraint of how React renders any component:
component functions must run synchronously to completion so React knows
what to output for that pass — there’s no way to pause mid-function and
resume later the way await would need to. use sidesteps this everywhere
it’s used by throwing instead of awaiting, letting React abandon and retry
the render rather than literally pausing execution. In our example, that’s
why ShipDetails can’t be async function ShipDetails() with an await
inside — it has to use() a promise instead.
Q: What happens if you create the promise fresh on every render, e.g. use(fetch(url))?
A: This is a general pitfall of use, independent of the specific data
being fetched: it re-triggers suspension forever, because each render
creates a brand-new promise object and use never sees “the same” promise
resolve. The promise must be created once — outside the component, via
props, or from a cache — and reused across renders. In our example,
shipPromise is created once outside ShipDetails (or, in the Promise Caching
version, memoized in a Map) specifically to avoid this trap:
// ❌ new promise every render — suspends forever
function Bad({ id }) {
  return use(fetch(`/ships/${id}`).then((r) =&gt; r.json()))
}

// ✅ created once, reused across renders
const shipPromise = getShip(shipName)
function Good() {
  return &lt;h2&gt;{use(shipPromise).name}&lt;/h2&gt;
}
Q: Is use subject to the Rules of Hooks (no conditionals/loops)?
A: No — this is a general, deliberate design difference from every other
hook: unlike useState/useContext, use can be called conditionally or
inside loops or after early returns, because it isn’t a Hook in the classic
sense (it doesn’t rely on stable call-order between renders the way real
Hooks do). Our example doesn’t exercise this flexibility — use(shipPromise)
is called unconditionally at the top of ShipDetails — but it’s a real,
generally-available capability.
Q: Can you wrap use in your own try/catch?
A: No, and this holds everywhere use is called: it relies on throwing
internally to integrate with Suspense/error boundaries, so catching that
throw yourself defeats the mechanism and silently breaks both the loading
and error UI. Handle rejections declaratively with an ErrorBoundary
instead, as our example does — see
Suspense and ErrorBoundary for Data Fetching.
use reads a resource during render. It has two unrelated jobs depending on
what you pass it: reading a context (see use and createContext) or
reading a Promise — which is what this note covers, and what powers
Suspense-based data fetching:
function ShipDetails({ shipPromise }: { shipPromise: Promise&lt;Ship&gt; }) {
  const ship = use(shipPromise) // suspends until shipPromise settles
  return &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;
}
There’s no await anywhere. use returns the resolved value synchronously
— the component function still runs top-to-bottom in one pass, it just
sometimes doesn’t get to finish that pass. How it pulls that off is the
interesting part.
The trick: you can’t await inside a component, but you can throw

Lesson video

JavaScript trivia: how do you synchronously stop a function from running to
completion, without returning early? You throw. That’s exactly what use
does with a pending promise, and React is set up to catch it.
Conceptually — React wraps the rendering of your component in a
try/catch:
// pseudocode for what React does around every component render
try {
  renderComponent()
} catch (thrown) {
  if (thrown instanceof Promise) {
    // &quot;suspending&quot;: attach a handler so React knows when to retry
    thrown.then(
      (value) =&gt; { /* record the resolved value */ },
      (reason) =&gt; { /* record the rejection reason */ },
    )
    renderNearestSuspenseFallback()
    // once `thrown` settles, React re-renders this component from scratch
  } else {
    renderNearestErrorBoundary(thrown) // a real error, not a promise
  }
}
And use(promise), given that same promise, does the other half:

Already fulfilled → return the resolved value synchronously. The
component keeps rendering normally.
Already rejected → throw the rejection reason. React’s catch
sees a non-promise thrown value and hands it to the nearest
ErrorBoundary (see Suspense and ErrorBoundary for Data Fetching).
Still pending → attach a .then to record the eventual result or
reason on the promise itself, then throw the promise. React’s catch
sees a promise, renders the nearest &lt;Suspense&gt; fallback, and — once that
attached .then fires — re-renders the component. This time, case 1 or 2
applies.


💬 This depends entirely on JavaScript Promises’s “a promise is just a
value” fact — use throws the promise object itself, not an error
about the promise. And it depends on the microtask-queue timing too:
the throw unwinds the current render synchronously, so React’s catch
and the fallback render happen before the promise’s own .then callback
ever gets a chance to run.
💬 That try/catch above is not just a teaching metaphor — React’s fiber
reconciler really does wrap its render work loop in one, and really does
branch on “is the thrown value thenable?” to decide Suspense vs. Error
Boundary. See Suspense and ErrorBoundary for Data Fetching &gt; How React actually implements this (fiber internals)
for the actual mechanism (throwException, attachPingListener, the
ShouldCapture/DidCapture flags).
💬 Is this wrapper always there, or only when a component is wrapped in
&lt;Suspense&gt;/&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;? Always there. The reconciler’s render
work loop wraps every component render unconditionally — it doesn’t
check for a nearby boundary before deciding whether to catch. Catching is
unconditional; what’s conditional is only what happens after catching:
React walks up the fiber tree looking for the nearest boundary that can
handle this particular kind of throw (thenable → nearest Suspense, real
error → nearest ErrorBoundary). If no matching boundary exists anywhere
up to the root, the throw goes unhandled — an uncaught error crashes/
unmounts the app (surfaced via onUncaughtError/the dev red-box), and a
suspended promise with no Suspense ancestor has nowhere to render a
fallback and surfaces as an error too. So “wrap it in Suspense/
ErrorBoundary” is about having a handler ready, not about whether React
is watching for throws in the first place — it always is.

Two .then consumers on one promise
Look closely at the pseudocode above and at the hand-rolled use below, and
there are two separate .then calls attached to the same promise —
React’s own, and use’s own. They’re easy to mistake for redundant, but
they do two different jobs:
// React&#039;s — a PING: &quot;wake me up when this settles so I can retry
// rendering this subtree.&quot; It doesn&#039;t need to expose the value anywhere
// your code can read. (Internally: attachPingListener.)
thrown.then(
  (value) =&gt; { /* record the resolved value */ },
  (reason) =&gt; { /* record the rejection reason */ },
)
// use&#039;s — a MEMOIZATION WRITE: stash the outcome directly on the promise
// object, so the NEXT call to use() on this same promise (the one React&#039;s
// ping is about to trigger) can answer synchronously instead of throwing
// again.
usePromise.then(
  (result) =&gt; { usePromise.status = &#039;fulfilled&#039;; usePromise.value = result },
  (reason) =&gt; { usePromise.status = &#039;rejected&#039;; usePromise.reason = reason },
)
Per JavaScript Promises’s note on multiple independent .then
subscribers, both can sit on the same promise without interfering — each
just fires, in the order it was attached, once the promise settles. And
each is necessary: React’s ping without use’s memoization would retry
render into an infinite throw loop (nothing ever moves use out of the
pending branch); use’s memoization without React’s ping would leave the
fallback on screen forever, since nothing schedules a retry.
Attachment order matters here, and it’s not accidental: use’s tracking
.then is attached first — synchronously, inside use, before it throws.
React’s ping .then is attached second — only after React’s catch
receives that throw. Microtasks run in attachment order, so on settle,
use’s callback writes status/value before React’s ping callback
fires and schedules the retry. That ordering is what makes case 3 below
safe: by the time the retry re-renders the component, the promise’s
status field is already up to date.

💬 Why does use have to track state manually at all? Because a
promise’s outcome isn’t synchronously readable — per
JavaScript Promises, the only way to learn it is asynchronously, via
.then. But use has to answer synchronously on the retry pass (no
await, remember). So something has to capture that asynchronous outcome
into a synchronously-readable place the instant it’s known — that’s what
writing status/value/reason as own-properties on the promise
accomplishes. It’s not use being sloppy; it’s the only way to bridge an
inherently-async result back into a synchronous read. (The &gt; 💬 note
under “Building it by hand” below covers how React’s real implementation
keeps this cache off the promise object itself, but for the same reason.)

Building it by hand (the workshop’s exercise)
To track a promise’s status across re-renders, the workshop monkey-patches
status/value/reason fields directly onto the promise object — hacky,
but it’s the simplest way to give the same promise a memory of its own
outcome across multiple render attempts:
type UsePromise&lt;Value&gt; = Promise&lt;Value&gt; &amp; {
  status: &#039;pending&#039; | &#039;fulfilled&#039; | &#039;rejected&#039;
  value: Value
  reason: unknown
}

function use&lt;Value&gt;(promise: Promise&lt;Value&gt;): Value {
  const usePromise = promise as UsePromise&lt;Value&gt;

  if (usePromise.status === &#039;fulfilled&#039;) {
    return usePromise.value
  } else if (usePromise.status === &#039;rejected&#039;) {
    throw usePromise.reason
  } else if (usePromise.status === &#039;pending&#039;) {
    throw usePromise
  } else {
    // first time we&#039;ve seen this promise: kick off tracking
    usePromise.status = &#039;pending&#039;
    usePromise.then(
      (result) =&gt; {
        usePromise.status = &#039;fulfilled&#039;
        usePromise.value = result
      },
      (reason) =&gt; {
        usePromise.status = &#039;rejected&#039;
        usePromise.reason = reason
      },
    )
    throw usePromise
  }
}
Which turns the component down to one line:
const shipPromise = getShip(shipName) // created once, outside the component

function ShipDetails() {
  const ship = use(shipPromise)
  return &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;
}
Then swapping the hand-rolled use for React’s real one is a no-op change
— same call site, same behavior:
import { use } from &#039;react&#039;
// (delete the hand-rolled `use` function entirely)

💬 React’s real implementation is more robust (it doesn’t literally mutate
your promise object the same way, and integrates with concurrent
rendering/transitions), but the shape of the algorithm — check status,
return/throw/throw-and-attach — is exactly this.

Mounting a component that calls use — step by step
Putting React’s pseudocode and the hand-rolled use together, here’s the
whole mount traced as one annotated run, across both render passes:
// STEP 0 — setup: promise created once, outside the component, so it
// survives across re-renders instead of being recreated every pass.
const shipPromise = getShip(&#039;Interceptor&#039;)

function ShipDetails() {
  const ship = use(shipPromise) // see STEP 2, then STEP 9
  return &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;
}

// ── First render pass (the actual mount) ────────────────────────────────

// STEP 1 — React&#039;s reconciler enters its unconditional try/catch and
// calls ShipDetails() for the first time.
try {
  renderComponent() // → calls ShipDetails()

  // STEP 2 — inside ShipDetails, use(shipPromise) runs. shipPromise has
  // no `status` field yet, so it falls into the untracked branch:
  //   usePromise.status = &#039;pending&#039;
  //   usePromise.then(                       // consumer #1 — attached FIRST
  //     (result) =&gt; { usePromise.status = &#039;fulfilled&#039;; usePromise.value = result },
  //     (reason) =&gt; { usePromise.status = &#039;rejected&#039;; usePromise.reason = reason },
  //   )
  //   throw usePromise

  // STEP 3 — that throw unwinds ShipDetails synchronously. The
  // `return &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;` line never runs this pass.
} catch (thrown) {
  if (thrown instanceof Promise) {
    // STEP 4 — React recognizes a thenable: this is a suspend, not an
    // error, so it does NOT go to renderNearestErrorBoundary.

    // STEP 5 — React attaches its OWN .then to the SAME promise —
    // consumer #2, attached SECOND — purely to get pinged on settle.
    thrown.then(
      (value) =&gt; { /* record the resolved value */ },
      (reason) =&gt; { /* record the rejection reason */ },
    )

    // STEP 6 — React walks up to the nearest &lt;Suspense&gt; and commits its
    // fallback. This is a REAL commit — it&#039;s what the user sees first.
    renderNearestSuspenseFallback()

    // STEP 7 — time passes. The async work behind shipPromise settles,
    // e.g. resolve(ship). Both attached .then callbacks are now queued as
    // microtasks, IN ATTACHMENT ORDER:
    //   (a) use&#039;s tracking .then (STEP 2) runs first  → status = &#039;fulfilled&#039;, value = ship
    //   (b) React&#039;s ping .then (STEP 5) runs second   → schedules a retry of ShipDetails
  } else {
    renderNearestErrorBoundary(thrown) // a real error, not a promise — not this path
  }
}

// ── Second render pass (the retry React&#039;s ping scheduled in STEP 7b) ───

// STEP 8 — React re-enters the SAME try/catch and calls ShipDetails()
// again, from scratch.
try {
  renderComponent() // → calls ShipDetails() again

  // STEP 9 — use(shipPromise) runs again. This time:
  //   usePromise.status === &#039;fulfilled&#039;   (written back in STEP 7a)
  //   → return usePromise.value           // synchronous — no throw

  // STEP 10 — ShipDetails finishes rendering with real data this time:
  //   &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;
} catch (thrown) {
  // not reached this pass — nothing was thrown
}

// STEP 11 — React reconciles the finished output against the fallback
// currently on screen and commits the swap: fallback disappears,
// &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt; appears.
Rules and caveats (from the official docs)

The promise must not be created fresh inside render. use(fetch(url))
directly in a component body creates a new promise on every render
attempt, which just re-triggers suspension forever. The promise has to be
created once — outside the component (as in the workshop above), passed
down via props, read from a cache, or produced by a Suspense-enabled
framework’s data layer.
use is not subject to the Rules of Hooks. Unlike useState or
useContext, use can be called conditionally or inside loops — see
use and createContext for why (it isn’t a Hook in the classic sense).
But don’t use that flexibility to manually branch on the promise’s own
status before calling use — always hand the promise straight to use
and let it decide; manually checking promise.status === &#039;fulfilled&#039;
first bypasses React’s suspense-handling optimizations.
You cannot wrap use in your own try/catch. It relies on throwing
internally to integrate with Suspense/error boundaries; catching that
throw yourself defeats the mechanism. Handle rejections declaratively with
an ErrorBoundary instead.
Cancellation isn’t automatic. If the user navigates away while a
promise is still pending, React doesn’t abort the underlying fetch for
you — nothing about use or Suspense touches the network request itself,
it only reacts to the promise settling. Wire up an AbortController
yourself (or rely on a data-fetching library that does) if wasted requests
matter.
Don’t mix use with useEffect for the same data. They’re two
different data-fetching models (declarative-suspend vs. imperative-fetch-
then-setState) — running both for one resource means double-fetching or
fighting over who owns the loading state. Pick one per resource.


💬 Client-side promise-reading with use shipped in React 19 initially
flagged as experimental in some early build channels while stabilizing
server-side first; by the time of this note it’s documented as a plain
stable API on react.dev with no experimental caveat. Worth a quick check
of your React version/changelog if you hit anything that suggests
otherwise.

