useOptimistic Hook

Notes from EpicWeb’s React Suspense
workshop by Kent C. Dodds (exercise 3, “Optimistic UI”, steps 1 and 3).
Assumes useTransition and Avoiding Loading Flicker — useOptimistic
only makes sense once you know what a transition is and why it hides
intermediate UI.

Interview Q&amp;A
Q: What is “optimistic UI”?
A: A general UI pattern usable in any app or framework: updating the screen
immediately, assuming an action will succeed (which it usually does),
rather than waiting for the server to confirm it first — e.g. a like button
turning red the instant you click it, before the network request even
resolves, or a chat message appearing right away before the server has
actually stored it. The point is to make the app feel instant even when
the network isn’t. In our example, a newly submitted ship appears on screen
immediately, before the create-and-fetch round trip actually finishes.
Q: Why doesn’t plain useState work for optimistic UI inside a form action?
A: This follows from a general rule about transitions, not something
specific to forms or ships: Form actions run inside a transition
automatically, and React holds back UI updates that happen during any
transition until it finishes — so a regular setState call wouldn’t render
until the whole action completes, defeating the point of showing something
immediately. This applies just as much to a startTransition(() =&gt; {...})
you write by hand as it does to a form action.
Q: How does useOptimistic solve this, and what’s its first argument?
A: useOptimistic is a general-purpose hook for any state that needs to
update during a transition, unlike useState. Its first argument isn’t
really an “initial” value — it’s the fallback value the state reverts to
once the transition ends, whether the action succeeded or failed. In our
example, that fallback is null (no optimistic ship) and, for the
multi-step message, the string &#039;Create&#039;:
const [optimisticShip, setOptimisticShip] = useOptimistic&lt;Ship | null&gt;(null)
// ...
setOptimisticShip(createOptimisticShip(formData))
Q: How do you get automatic rollback on failure with useOptimistic?
A: This “rollback” behavior is a general property of the hook, not
something you implement yourself: once the transition ends, the optimistic
value disappears and the component re-renders based on the real data,
whatever it is. If the update failed (or the real data didn’t change), the
UI just reverts to whatever the real data still says — you get correctness
for free by falling back to the source of truth. In our example, if
creating the ship failed, optimisticShip would fall back to null and
ShipDetails would go back to suspending on the real (unchanged) ship list.
Q: Can you update optimistic state more than once during a single action?
A: Yes, and this generalizes to any multi-step async process (checkout
flows, multi-stage uploads, wizards) — since optimistic state is allowed to
render during the transition, you can call the setter multiple times to
show progress through a multi-step action, falling back to the initial
value once the whole action finishes. In our example, that’s the message
optimistic value stepping through “Creating…” → “Loading…”:
setMessage(&#039;Creating order...&#039;)
const order = await createOrder(formData)
setMessage(&#039;Creating payment...&#039;)
await createPayment(formData, order)
What “optimistic UI” means
Most user actions succeed. Optimistic UI leans into that: instead of waiting
for the server to confirm an action before updating the screen, you update
the screen immediately, assuming success, and only correct course if the
request actually fails. Liking a post on social media and seeing the heart
turn red instantly — before the network request even resolves — is the
classic example.
Why useState doesn’t work here
Form Actions (the action={...} prop on a &lt;form&gt;) run inside a
transition automatically. As covered in
useTransition and Avoiding Loading Flicker, React holds back UI updates
that happen during a transition until the transition finishes — that’s the
whole point of a transition, it’s what keeps the old UI visible while new
data is being fetched.
That means a plain setState call inside a form action won’t show up on
screen until the entire action finishes — which defeats the purpose of
optimistic UI entirely. You need a way to update state that’s allowed to
render during a transition. That’s what useOptimistic is for.
useOptimistic: state that updates during a transition, then falls back
useOptimistic looks like useState, but its first argument isn’t really
an “initial” value — it’s the fallback value the state reverts to once
the transition ends (whether it succeeded or failed):
function Todo({ todo }: { todo: TodoItem }) {
  const [isComplete, setIsComplete] = useOptimistic(todo.isComplete)

  return (
    &lt;form
      action={async () =&gt; {
        setIsComplete(!isComplete)
        await updateTodo(todo.id, !isComplete)
      }}
    &gt;
      &lt;label&gt;
        &lt;input type=&quot;checkbox&quot; checked={isComplete} readOnly /&gt;
        {todo.text}
      &lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  )
}
What happens on submit:

The form action runs inside a transition.
setIsComplete(!isComplete) updates the optimistic state — and unlike a
regular setState call, React does let this render immediately, so
the checkbox flips right away.
await updateTodo(...) does the real work in the background.
Once the transition finishes, the optimistic value disappears and the
component re-renders based on todo.isComplete again. If the update
succeeded and todo reflects the new state, nothing visibly changes. If
it failed (or todo didn’t update), the checkbox reverts — that’s the
“rollback” of optimistic UI, and you get it for free just by falling back
to real data.


📜 useOptimistic docs

Worked example: showing a ship before it’s actually saved
The workshop applies this to a “create ship” form. Submitting takes a
moment (upload + fetch the newly created record), so instead of showing
nothing until that finishes, an optimistic version of the ship is
displayed right away:
function App() {
  const [optimisticShip, setOptimisticShip] = useOptimistic&lt;Ship | null&gt;(null)

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;CreateForm setOptimisticShip={setOptimisticShip} /&gt;
      &lt;ShipDetails shipName={shipName} optimisticShip={optimisticShip} /&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  )
}

function CreateForm({ setOptimisticShip }: { setOptimisticShip: (s: Ship) =&gt; void }) {
  return (
    &lt;form
      action={async (formData) =&gt; {
        // createOptimisticShip builds a Ship-shaped object straight from
        // the form data — no server round-trip needed for this part.
        setOptimisticShip(createOptimisticShip(formData))
        await createShip(formData)
      }}
    &gt;
      {/* inputs... */}
    &lt;/form&gt;
  )
}

function ShipDetails({
  shipName,
  optimisticShip,
}: {
  shipName: string
  optimisticShip: Ship | null
}) {
  // Show the optimistic ship immediately if we have one; otherwise suspend
  // on the real fetch as usual.
  const ship = optimisticShip ?? use(getShip(shipName))
  return &lt;h2&gt;{ship.name}&lt;/h2&gt;
}
Some fields genuinely can’t be known yet (e.g. a server-assigned
fetchedAt timestamp) — createOptimisticShip fills those with a
placeholder (like &#039;...&#039;) rather than a fake value, so it’s honest with the
user about what’s still pending. Once the real create + fetch finishes and
the transition ends, optimisticShip falls back to null and ShipDetails
renders the real, server-confirmed ship instead.
Multi-step actions: updating optimistic state more than once
Because optimistic state is allowed to update during a transition, you’re
not limited to one update per action — you can call the setter multiple
times to reflect progress through a multi-step action:
&lt;form
  action={async (formData) =&gt; {
    setMessage(&#039;Creating order...&#039;)
    const order = await createOrder(formData)
    setMessage(&#039;Creating payment...&#039;)
    const payment = await createPayment(formData, order)
    setMessage(&#039;Almost done!&#039;)
    await sendThankYou(order, payment)
  }}
&gt;
  {/* ... */}
&lt;/form&gt;
message here is itself a useOptimistic(&#039;Create&#039;) value — initialized to
its resting/fallback text, then updated at each step. When the whole action
finishes, it falls back to &#039;Create&#039; again automatically, same as any other
optimistic value.

💬 useOptimistic also accepts a second argument: a reducer-style
function (currentState, optimisticValue) =&gt; newState, used when the
update needs to be merged into existing state rather than replacing it
outright (e.g. appending one new message to a chat history instead of
overwriting the whole list). Not covered in depth by this workshop, but
worth knowing the hook supports it.

