Data Fetching — Render Trace (Exercise 1)

Line-by-line render/execution trace through the 5 solution steps of
exercise 1 (“Data Fetching”) of EpicWeb’s
React Suspense
workshop. Same underlying ShipDetails app, evolving step by step from a
blank-screen await fetch to React’s built-in use. For the concept
explanations (not the trace) see JavaScript Promises, The use Hook,
and Suspense and ErrorBoundary for Data Fetching — this note is about
invocation order: what runs first, what throws, what catches, what
re-renders.

1.1 — Throwing Promises
Problem: the app awaits getShip(shipName) at module scope before
rendering anything, so the user stares at a blank white screen for the whole
request. Nothing renders until the fetch finishes.
import { Suspense } from &#039;react&#039;
import { getImageUrlForShip, getShip, type Ship } from &#039;./utils.tsx&#039;

const shipName = &#039;Dreadnought&#039;

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;app-wrapper&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div className=&quot;app&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div className=&quot;details&quot;&gt;
          &lt;Suspense fallback={&lt;ShipFallback /&gt;}&gt;
            &lt;ShipDetails /&gt;
          &lt;/Suspense&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

let ship: Ship
const shipPromise = getShip(shipName, 1000).then((result) =&gt; (ship = result))

function ShipDetails() {
  if (!ship) throw shipPromise

  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;ship-info&quot;&gt;
      {/* ...renders ship.name, image, etc. */}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

Module loads. getShip(shipName, 1000) is called immediately, not
awaited — the fetch starts right away and shipPromise holds it. A
.then is chained to assign the resolved value into the outer ship
variable once it lands (this is a closure-captured mutable variable, not
React state — nothing here triggers a re-render on its own).
ReactDOM renders &lt;App /&gt;. React walks the tree: div.app-wrapper →
div.app → div.details → &lt;Suspense fallback={&lt;ShipFallback /&gt;}&gt; →
&lt;ShipDetails /&gt;.
ShipDetails() runs. ship is still undefined (the fetch hasn’t
resolved yet), so if (!ship) throw shipPromise throws the promise
itself — not an error, a plain Promise value, because JS lets you
throw anything.
React’s render loop has an implicit boundary-aware catch around every
component render. It catches the thrown value, sees it’s a Promise (via
.then existing on it, structurally), and treats this as “this subtree
suspended.” It walks up the tree to the nearest &lt;Suspense&gt; ancestor
and commits ShipFallback in its place instead of ShipDetails.
React also attaches its own .then handler to the thrown shipPromise so
it knows when to retry.
Time passes. The original .then (added in step 1) resolves first (it was
attached first in source order) and assigns ship = result. React’s own
.then handler then fires and tells React “this promise settled, try
rendering that subtree again.”
React re-renders ShipDetails(). This time ship is defined, if (!ship) is false, so execution falls through and the actual ship info
renders. &lt;Suspense&gt; swaps the fallback back out for the real children.

Why
Removing the top-level await is what lets React get to the render call
at all — synchronous rendering can’t wait on a promise, so the only way to
communicate “I’m not ready” from inside a component during render is to
throw. Throwing the promise (rather than, say, returning null) is what
lets React specifically know it’s a suspension, not a normal error, so it
can show fallback and retry later instead of crashing to an error
boundary.
1.2 — Error Handling
Problem: the happy path suspends and resolves fine, but a failed fetch
(bad ship name, network error) leaves the promise rejected and the app stuck
showing the ShipFallback forever — no feedback for the user, and an
uncaught rejection in the console.
import { ErrorBoundary } from &#039;react-error-boundary&#039;

function App() {
  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;app-wrapper&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div className=&quot;app&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div className=&quot;details&quot;&gt;
          &lt;ErrorBoundary fallback={&lt;ShipError /&gt;}&gt;
            &lt;Suspense fallback={&lt;ShipFallback /&gt;}&gt;
              &lt;ShipDetails /&gt;
            &lt;/Suspense&gt;
          &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

let ship: Ship
let error: unknown
const shipPromise = getShip(shipName).then(
  (result) =&gt; (ship = result),
  (err) =&gt; (error = err),
)

function ShipDetails() {
  if (error) throw error
  if (!ship) throw shipPromise
  // ...
}
Render trace

Same start as 1.1: fetch kicks off immediately, but now .then has
two handlers — success assigns ship, failure assigns error.
ShipDetails() renders. Neither ship nor error is set yet →
if (error) throw error is skipped, if (!ship) throw shipPromise fires
→ same suspend-to-&lt;Suspense&gt; path as before, ShipFallback shows.
The fetch rejects (e.g. ship not found → 404). The rejection handler runs,
sets error = err. React’s internal .then on the thrown promise also
fires (a promise only settles once, so both handlers — yours and React’s —
run off that single settlement) and tells React to retry the render.
ShipDetails() re-renders. Now if (error) throw error is true —
this time it’s an actual Error/rejection reason being thrown, a
genuinely different signal than a pending promise.
React’s render-loop catch sees this thrown value is not a promise (no
.then), so it’s not a suspension — it’s an error. It walks up to the
nearest &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt; (not &lt;Suspense&gt;) and commits that boundary’s
fallback, here &lt;ShipError /&gt;.

Why
&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt; has to wrap &lt;Suspense&gt;, not the other way around,
because the thing that eventually throws the error is the same component
(ShipDetails) that throws the pending promise — the two throws are
distinguished by what is thrown (promise vs. non-promise), and React needs
both a Suspense boundary and an error boundary in the ancestor chain to
route each kind of throw to the right fallback. Typing the caught value as
unknown (not Error) matters because, as the transcript notes, anything
can be thrown in JS — including a promise, which is exactly what’s
happening one line above.
1.3 — Formal Status
Problem: if (error) throw error; if (!ship) throw shipPromise works,
but the three real states (still loading / succeeded / failed) are being
inferred from the presence/absence of two separate variables rather than
named explicitly — an implicit state machine that’s easy to get wrong as it
grows.
let ship: Ship
let error: unknown
let status: &#039;pending&#039; | &#039;rejected&#039; | &#039;fulfilled&#039; = &#039;pending&#039;
const shipPromise = getShip(shipName).then(
  (result) =&gt; {
    ship = result
    status = &#039;fulfilled&#039;
  },
  (err) =&gt; {
    error = err
    status = &#039;rejected&#039;
  },
)

function ShipDetails() {
  if (status === &#039;rejected&#039;) throw error
  if (status === &#039;pending&#039;) throw shipPromise
  // status must be &#039;fulfilled&#039; here — ship is guaranteed to be set
  // ...
}
Render trace
Identical control flow to 1.2 — same suspend-then-resolve-or-reject
sequence — but now driven off one explicit status variable instead of two
implicit signals (!ship, error truthiness). status starts &#039;pending&#039;;
ShipDetails throws the promise while it’s &#039;pending&#039;, throws error once
it flips to &#039;rejected&#039;, and falls through to rendering once it’s
&#039;fulfilled&#039; — nothing about when React suspends/errors/renders changes,
only how clearly the code states which of the three cases it’s in.
Why
This is a pure refactor with no behavior change — its value is legibility
and correctness-by-construction. It’s a direct application of Kent C.
Dodds’s Make Impossible States
Impossible
and Stop using isLoading
booleans: with
two independent booleans/nullable variables you can theoretically end up in
a state that shouldn’t exist (e.g. both an error and a ship set); a single
status enum makes the three valid states the only representable states.
It also sets up the exact shape (`use`‘s internal
status/value/reason) that step 1.4 formalizes into a reusable utility.
1.4 — Utility
Problem: the pending/fulfilled/rejected-throwing dance from 1.1–1.3 is
duplicated per data-fetching component. Extract it into a single reusable
use(promise) function.
type UsePromise&lt;Value&gt; = Promise&lt;Value&gt; &amp; {
  status: &#039;pending&#039; | &#039;fulfilled&#039; | &#039;rejected&#039;
  value: Value
  reason: unknown
}

function use&lt;Value&gt;(promise: Promise&lt;Value&gt;): Value {
  const usePromise = promise as UsePromise&lt;Value&gt;
  if (usePromise.status === &#039;fulfilled&#039;) {
    return usePromise.value
  } else if (usePromise.status === &#039;rejected&#039;) {
    throw usePromise.reason
  } else if (usePromise.status === &#039;pending&#039;) {
    throw usePromise
  } else {
    usePromise.status = &#039;pending&#039;
    usePromise.then(
      (result) =&gt; {
        usePromise.status = &#039;fulfilled&#039;
        usePromise.value = result
      },
      (reason) =&gt; {
        usePromise.status = &#039;rejected&#039;
        usePromise.reason = reason
      },
    )
    throw usePromise
  }
}

const shipPromise = getShip(shipName)

function ShipDetails() {
  const ship = use(shipPromise)
  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;ship-info&quot;&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

ShipDetails() calls use(shipPromise). Inside use, the promise is
cast to UsePromise — a hack: the workshop monkey-patches status,
value, and reason directly onto the promise object itself, so
there’s exactly one object carrying both “the async operation” and “its
current state,” instead of three separate closure variables like 1.1–1.3.
First call: usePromise.status is undefined (none of the three known
strings) → falls to the else branch. Sets status = &#039;pending&#039;,
attaches .then handlers that will mutate status/value/reason on
this same promise object when it settles, then throw usePromise.
Same suspend path as before: React catches the thrown promise, renders
the &lt;Suspense&gt; fallback, and waits.
Promise settles → the handler set up in step 2 fires, mutating
usePromise.status to &#039;fulfilled&#039; (with .value) or &#039;rejected&#039;
(with .reason) — this mutation happens on the promise object outside of
any render. React’s own attached listener (from catching the throw)
fires and triggers a re-render.
ShipDetails() calls use(shipPromise) again. Now
usePromise.status === &#039;fulfilled&#039; (or &#039;rejected&#039;) is true on the very
first branch checked, so use either returns usePromise.value
synchronously (component renders normally) or throws
usePromise.reason (caught by the nearest &lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;, same as
1.2/1.3).

Why
Everything from 1.1–1.3 was really one pattern — “throw while pending, throw
on reject, return on fulfilled” — repeated inline. Formalizing it as use
collapses each data-fetching component down to one line
(const ship = use(shipPromise)) and moves the pending/error mechanics to a
single, testable place. It’s also a deliberate pedagogical step: build a
simplified version of the real thing by hand so the mechanism is no longer a
black box before step 1.5 swaps in the real one.
1.5 — use React
Problem: now that the hand-rolled use utility’s behavior is
understood, replace it with React’s own built-in use.
import { Suspense, use } from &#039;react&#039;
import { getImageUrlForShip, getShip } from &#039;./utils.tsx&#039;

const shipName = &#039;Dreadnought&#039;
const shipPromise = getShip(shipName)

function ShipDetails() {
  const ship = use(shipPromise)
  // ...
}
The entire UsePromise type and hand-rolled use function from 1.4 are
deleted; the import switches to React’s built-in use.
Render trace
Behaviorally identical to 1.4’s trace — suspend while pending (caught by
&lt;Suspense&gt;), throw the rejection reason on failure (caught by
&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;), return the value synchronously once fulfilled. The only
difference is where the pending/fulfilled/rejected bookkeeping lives: React’s
internal fiber/cache implementation instead of properties monkey-patched
directly onto the promise in userland. Same call site
(const ship = use(shipPromise)), same throw/catch mechanics from React’s
perspective.
Why
This confirms the mental model built across 1.1–1.4 wasn’t just an analogy —
it’s actually how React’s real use behaves under the hood (throw the
promise while pending, throw the reason on rejection, return the value on
fulfillment). Building the naive version first, then deleting it for the
real one, is what makes the built-in use legible instead of magic. See
The use Hook for React’s actual internal implementation details (fiber
dependencies, the render-phase try/catch) beyond what this trace covers.
