Exercise 3 — Optimistic UI: Render Trace

This note traces the render and invocation order across all 3 steps of
exercise 3 (Optimistic UI), using the actual solution code from each step.
It complements — doesn’t replace — the conceptual deep-dives in
useOptimistic Hook and useFormStatus; those explain the APIs in
general, this note walks through exactly what fires, in what order, and
why, as the workshop’s ship-creation form runs.

Step 3.1 — Optimistic UI
Problem
Creating a ship takes a network round-trip (upload the form, then fetch the
newly created ship), and until now the user sees nothing change until both
finish. The goal: show a stand-in version of the new ship immediately on
submit.
Code
import { Suspense, use, useOptimistic, useState, useTransition } from &#039;react&#039;
import { type Ship, getShip, createShip } from &#039;./utils.tsx&#039;

function App() {
  const [shipName, setShipName] = useState(&#039;Dreadnought&#039;)
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition()
  const [optimisticShip, setOptimisticShip] = useOptimistic&lt;Ship | null&gt;(null)

  function handleShipSelection(newShipName: string) {
    startTransition(() =&gt; {
      setShipName(newShipName)
    })
  }

  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;app-wrapper&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div className=&quot;app&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div className=&quot;details&quot; style={{ opacity: isPending ? 0.6 : 1 }}&gt;
          &lt;ErrorBoundary fallback={&lt;ShipError shipName={shipName} /&gt;}&gt;
            &lt;Suspense fallback={&lt;ShipFallback shipName={shipName} /&gt;}&gt;
              &lt;ShipDetails shipName={shipName} optimisticShip={optimisticShip} /&gt;
            &lt;/Suspense&gt;
          &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;CreateForm setOptimisticShip={setOptimisticShip} setShipName={setShipName} /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

function CreateForm({
  setOptimisticShip,
  setShipName,
}: {
  setOptimisticShip: (ship: Ship | null) =&gt; void
  setShipName: (name: string) =&gt; void
}) {
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Create a new ship&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={FormErrorFallback}&gt;
        &lt;form
          action={async (formData) =&gt; {
            setOptimisticShip(await createOptimisticShip(formData))
            await createShip(formData, 2000)
            setShipName(formData.get(&#039;name&#039;) as string)
          }}
        &gt;
          {/* inputs... */}
          &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Create&lt;/button&gt;
        &lt;/form&gt;
      &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

function ShipDetails({
  shipName,
  optimisticShip,
}: {
  shipName: string
  optimisticShip: Ship | null
}) {
  const delay = 2000
  const ship = optimisticShip ?? use(getShip(shipName, delay))
  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;ship-info&quot;&gt;
      &lt;div className=&quot;ship-info__img-wrapper&quot;&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

App renders optimisticShip (from useOptimistic&lt;Ship | null&gt;(null), so it starts as null) and passes it straight through to ShipDetails.
ShipDetails evaluates optimisticShip ?? use(getShip(shipName, delay)). With optimisticShip still null, use(...) runs as normal — either resolves synchronously (already cached) or throws the pending promise, exactly as in useTransition and Avoiding Loading Flicker.
User submits the form. The &lt;form action={...}&gt; function is a React Action — it automatically runs inside a transition (same low-priority-render machinery useTransition uses, but implicit here).
Line 1 inside the action: setOptimisticShip(await createOptimisticShip(formData)). createOptimisticShip synchronously builds a Ship-shaped object out of the submitted FormData (no network call — fetchedAt is stubbed to &#039;...&#039; since the real timestamp doesn’t exist yet). Because setOptimisticShip is a useOptimistic setter and this call happens inside the Action, React lets this update render immediately, even though the surrounding Action is a transition that would otherwise hold updates back — this is the one exception useOptimistic carves out.
That immediate re-render flows back into ShipDetails: optimisticShip is now non-null, so ship = optimisticShip — the use(getShip(...)) branch is short-circuited entirely and never suspends for this render. The user sees the fabricated ship instantly.
Line 2: await createShip(formData, 2000) — the real network request runs in the background. Nothing on screen changes while this awaits; the optimistic ship is already showing.
Line 3: setShipName(formData.get(&#039;name&#039;)) — a plain useState update, not optimistic. Because it’s still inside the same Action/transition, this update is not shown immediately — it’s held back until the transition (the whole async action function) finishes.
When the Action’s promise settles, the transition ends. Two things resolve together: optimisticShip falls back to its initial value (null, since the Action is done), and the deferred setShipName update finally commits. ShipDetails re-renders with optimisticShip = null again, so it falls through to use(getShip(newShipName, delay)) — which, since createShip already finished, likely resolves the cached/fresh promise quickly (still possibly suspending briefly under &lt;Suspense&gt;/isPending, per exercise 2’s caching + transition machinery).

Why
A plain useState call for the optimistic ship wouldn’t render until the whole Action finished — defeating the purpose. useOptimistic is the one state primitive allowed to render during an in-flight transition/Action, which is exactly the gap this step fills. Rollback is automatic and “free”: once the transition ends, the optimistic value always falls back to its initial argument, so nothing extra needs to be written for the case where the create fails.
Step 3.2 — Form Status
Problem
Nothing stops the user from clicking “Create” repeatedly while the first submission is still in flight, re-triggering the Action multiple times, and there’s no visible feedback that a submission is in progress.
Code
import { useFormStatus } from &#039;react-dom&#039;

function CreateForm({ /* ... */ }) {
  return (
    &lt;form action={/* ... */}&gt;
      {/* inputs... */}
      &lt;CreateButton /&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
  )
}

function CreateButton() {
  const { pending } = useFormStatus()
  return (
    &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot; disabled={pending}&gt;
      {pending ? &#039;Creating...&#039; : &#039;Create&#039;}
    &lt;/button&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

The submit &lt;button&gt; is pulled out of CreateForm into its own CreateButton component, rendered as a child of the &lt;form&gt;.
This is a hard requirement, not a style choice: useFormStatus looks up the DOM tree for the nearest ancestor &lt;form&gt;’s status. If CreateButton’s logic stayed inline in CreateForm — the component that renders the &lt;form&gt; — there’d be no ancestor &lt;form&gt; to find yet at that point in the render, since CreateForm is a sibling context to the form it’s building, not a descendant of it.
Before any submission: useFormStatus() returns { pending: false, data: null, method: null, action: null }, so the button reads “Create” and is enabled.
On submit, React starts the form Action (the same one from step 3.1) and, in the same render pass that starts it, updates every useFormStatus() call reading from that &lt;form&gt; to pending: true with data set to the live FormData. Note this happens independently of — and before — anything the Action’s own body does (setOptimisticShip, createShip, etc.); it’s automatic bookkeeping React does for any form with an Action.
CreateButton re-renders with pending: true, so the button reads “Creating…” and becomes disabled — blocking repeat clicks while the Action is still running.
When the Action’s promise settles and the transition ends, pending flips back to false in the same “transition finishes” batch described in step 3.1 (point 8) — CreateButton, ShipDetails, and App’s shipName state all update together.

Why
useFormStatus needs zero state wiring — it’s read-only, derived automatically from the nearest &lt;form&gt;’s in-flight Action. The child-of-&lt;form&gt; requirement is why extracting a submit button into its own component is such a common pattern: it’s the only way to get underneath the form to read its status.
Step 3.3 — Multi-step Actions
Problem
The form Action actually does two things — create the ship, then trigger loading it — but the button only ever says “Creating…”, with no indication of which sub-step is currently happening.
Code
function CreateForm({
  setOptimisticShip,
  setShipName,
}: {
  setOptimisticShip: (ship: Ship | null) =&gt; void
  setShipName: (name: string) =&gt; void
}) {
  const [message, setMessage] = useOptimistic(&#039;Create&#039;)
  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;Create a new ship&lt;/p&gt;
      &lt;ErrorBoundary FallbackComponent={FormErrorFallback}&gt;
        &lt;form
          action={async (formData) =&gt; {
            setMessage(&#039;Creating...&#039;)
            setOptimisticShip(await createOptimisticShip(formData))

            await createShip(formData, 2000)

            setMessage(&#039;Created! Loading...&#039;)
            setShipName(formData.get(&#039;name&#039;) as string)
          }}
        &gt;
          {/* inputs... */}
          &lt;CreateButton&gt;{message}&lt;/CreateButton&gt;
        &lt;/form&gt;
      &lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  )
}

function CreateButton({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const { pending } = useFormStatus()
  return (
    &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot; disabled={pending}&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/button&gt;
  )
}
Render trace

CreateForm now owns a second optimistic value, message, initialized to &#039;Create&#039; — its resting/fallback text.
CreateButton no longer decides its own text from pending; it just renders whatever children (the message value) it’s handed, while still using pending only to control disabled. This decouples “is the form busy” (useFormStatus, step 3.2) from “what should the button say right now” (useOptimistic, this step) — two independent concerns that happen to update in lockstep here.
On submit, inside the Action, in order:

setMessage(&#039;Creating...&#039;) — renders immediately (same “optimistic updates render during the transition” exception from step 3.1). Button now reads “Creating…”.
setOptimisticShip(await createOptimisticShip(formData)) — same as step 3.1, the fabricated ship appears in ShipDetails. Both optimistic updates happen in close succession but are independent useOptimistic calls — each can be called and rendered as many times as needed during the same Action.
await createShip(formData, 2000) — real request in flight; both message and optimisticShip stay at their latest optimistic values while this awaits.
setMessage(&#039;Created! Loading...&#039;) — renders immediately again, updating the button text mid-Action without waiting for anything else.
setShipName(...) — as in step 3.1, this is a plain useState update and is not shown until the Action/transition finishes.


When the Action’s promise resolves and the transition ends: both message and optimisticShip fall back to their initial values (&#039;Create&#039; and null) in the same commit that finally applies the deferred setShipName — button reverts to “Create” right as ShipDetails starts evaluating the new shipName for real (possibly suspending/pending per exercises 1-2’s machinery).

Why
Any useOptimistic call — not just one per component — is allowed to update state during a transition, so a single Action can drive a sequence of provisional UI states (a progress narrative) just by calling multiple setters, or the same setter multiple times, at each logical step. This generalizes beyond forms to any multi-step async process (checkout flows, multi-stage uploads): the mental model is simply “does this state need to be visible during an in-flight transition — if yes, it can’t be useState, it has to be useOptimistic.”
Sources

EpicReact — Optimistic UI (exercise + solution)
EpicReact — Form Status (exercise + solution)
EpicReact — Multi-step Actions (exercise + solution)
react.dev — useOptimistic
react.dev — useFormStatus

Exercise 5 — Responsive: render trace

Traces render/execution order for exercise 5’s single step. This one is
about useDeferredValue, which solves a specific problem
`useTransition` can’t: keeping
an input responsive to typing while the results it drives still suspend
and show a pending state.

5.1 — useDeferredValue (solution video)
The problem
The ship search filter uses the same useTransition pattern as the ship
picker from exercise 2: setSearch is wrapped in startTransition so the
suspending &lt;SearchResults&gt; doesn’t blow away the UI. But a transition
defers the whole state update, including the parts that don’t suspend —
so while isPending is true, the &lt;input&gt;’s own value={search} doesn’t
update either. Typing feels broken: characters don’t appear until the
transition finishes.
The code
Before (problem — everything, including the input value, is inside the transition):
const [isTransitionPending, startTransition] = useTransition()
const isPending = useSpinDelay(isTransitionPending, {
	delay: 300,
	minDuration: 350,
})

// ...
&lt;input
	value={search}
	onChange={(event) =&gt; {
		startTransition(() =&gt; setSearch(event.currentTarget.value))
	}}
/&gt;
// ...
&lt;SearchResults search={search} onSelection={onSelection} /&gt;
After (solution):
import { Suspense, use, useDeferredValue, useState, useTransition } from &#039;react&#039;

function ShipSearch({ onSelection }: { onSelection: (shipName: string) =&gt; void }) {
	const [search, setSearch] = useState(&#039;&#039;)
	const deferredSearch = useDeferredValue(search)
	const isPending = useSpinDelay(search !== deferredSearch)

	return (
		&lt;&gt;
			&lt;div&gt;
				&lt;input
					type=&quot;search&quot;
					value={search}
					onChange={(event) =&gt; {
						setSearch(event.currentTarget.value)
					}}
				/&gt;
			&lt;/div&gt;
			&lt;ErrorBoundary fallback={&lt;SearchResultsFallback /&gt;}&gt;
				&lt;ul style={{ opacity: isPending ? 0.6 : 1 }}&gt;
					&lt;Suspense fallback={&lt;SearchResultsFallback /&gt;}&gt;
						&lt;SearchResults search={deferredSearch} onSelection={onSelection} /&gt;
					&lt;/Suspense&gt;
				&lt;/ul&gt;
			&lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;
		&lt;/&gt;
	)
}

function SearchResults({
	search,
	onSelection,
}: {
	search: string
	onSelection: (shipName: string) =&gt; void
}) {
	const shipResults = use(searchShips(search, 500))
	return shipResults.ships.map((ship) =&gt; (
		&lt;li key={ship.name}&gt;
			&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; onSelection(ship.name)}&gt;{/* ... */}&lt;/button&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
	))
}
Three changes, all in ShipSearch — SearchResults itself is untouched:

setSearch(event.currentTarget.value) is called directly in
onChange — no startTransition wrapper.
const deferredSearch = useDeferredValue(search) replaces useTransition.
isPending is now derived from search !== deferredSearch (fed through
useSpinDelay to avoid a flash), instead of useTransition’s isPending
boolean. &lt;SearchResults search={deferredSearch} .../&gt; gets the deferred
value, not the live one.

Render trace
Type a character, e.g. the user types n into a currently-empty input.

onChange fires → setSearch(&#039;n&#039;) runs as a normal, urgent state
update (nothing wraps it — this is the whole point). React re-renders
ShipSearch immediately and synchronously commits it.
On this immediate render, search is &#039;n&#039;, but useDeferredValue(search)
returns the previous value it had — here, &#039;&#039; — because React hasn’t
had a chance to compute the deferred version yet. So deferredSearch is
still &#039;&#039; on this render.
Because search (&#039;n&#039;) !== deferredSearch (&#039;&#039;), isPending becomes
true (through useSpinDelay), and &lt;SearchResults search={deferredSearch}&gt;
is still called with the old &#039;&#039; value — so it renders whatever it
already rendered for an empty search (no suspend, since that promise is
presumably already cached/resolved). Nothing new suspends on this
render — that’s the key: the urgent render (the one that makes the
input feel responsive) never touches the new, potentially-suspending data.
Committed to the screen: the &lt;input&gt; shows n right away (input is
never deferred, only search’s downstream consumer is), and the results
list is dimmed to opacity: 0.6 because isPending is true. This
commit is fast and never blocked by searchShips.
Immediately after that commit, React starts a second, background
render of ShipSearch with deferredSearch now caught up to &#039;n&#039; (this
is what “React branches and renders your component in two instances”
means in the transcript — not two components on screen, but two render
passes for the same subtree at two different priorities).
In that background render, &lt;SearchResults search=&quot;n&quot;&gt; calls
use(searchShips(&#039;n&#039;, 500)). If that promise is still pending, use
throws it, &lt;SearchResults&gt; suspends — but because this is a background
(deferred) render, not a synchronous one, React does not show the
&lt;Suspense fallback&gt;. It just keeps the previous commit (step 4) on
screen and keeps retrying/waiting.
If the user types another character (&#039;a&#039;) before searchShips(&#039;n&#039;, 500)
resolves: step 1 repeats immediately with the new urgent value, the
in-flight background render for &#039;n&#039; is abandoned, and a new background
render starts for &#039;a&#039;, etc. This is the “interrupt the pending state by
typing more” behavior the problem README asks for — the deferred value is
always chasing whatever search most recently was, and only the latest
background render matters.
Once a background render actually resolves without suspending (say
searchShips(&#039;n&#039;, 500) finally resolves and the user hasn’t typed since),
React commits it: deferredSearch becomes &#039;n&#039;, search === deferredSearch
again so isPending flips back to false, and the results list
un-dims and shows the new ships.

Contrast with the useTransition version this replaced: there, step 1 itself
was deferred (the whole setSearch call ran inside startTransition), so the
input was part of the low-priority render and stayed stale until the
transition settled. useDeferredValue fixes this by only deferring the
value passed downstream, not the state update that drives the input —
the urgent render and the deferred render diverge only at the point where
deferredSearch is read.
Why useDeferredValue instead of useTransition here

useTransition/startTransition defers an update you trigger — you
choose to wrap setSearch(...) in it, so everything that update touches
(including the input’s own value) becomes low-priority together. That’s
fine when nothing needs to stay urgent, like the ship picker in exercise 2.
useDeferredValue defers a value you already have, independent of how
it changed. It doesn’t care whether search came from your own
setState, from props, from context, or from a URL param — it just lags
one render behind. That decoupling is exactly what’s needed here: search
itself must update urgently (so typing feels instant), while only the
consumer of that value (&lt;SearchResults&gt;, which does the expensive/
suspending work) should lag behind.
Per React’s own docs, this isn’t a fixed-delay debounce — useDeferredValue
has no timer. It starts the background render immediately after the urgent
one commits and always prioritizes further urgent (user-driven) updates
over finishing that background work, so on a fast device/fast network it
can feel almost instantaneous, and it naturally self-adjusts under load
instead of needing a hand-tuned debounce interval.
“Responsive” in this exercise’s title is literal: the fix is specifically
about keeping the input responsive to keystrokes while still getting a
correct pending indicator and Suspense-integrated loading behavior for the
results driven by that input.

Sources

EpicReact — useDeferredValue (exercise + solution)
react.dev — useDeferredValue
react.dev — useDeferredValue deep dive: how is deferring different from debouncing/throttling
useTransition and Avoiding Loading Flicker — the useTransition pattern this step replaces, and why it doesn’t work when part of the update (the input value) must stay urgent

