claude/06-rag-and-agentic-search/05-impl.content:
Implementing the RAG flow



                  Course context 



Part of RAG and Agentic Search — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
This lesson turns the conceptual RAG flow into real, runnable code — chunking actual documents, storing embeddings somewhere searchable, and wiring retrieval up to a Claude API call. It’s the hands-on counterpart to the previous, more theoretical lesson, showing the practical decisions (which library, how to structure the code) that a working prototype requires.
Deep dive
A concrete RAG implementation needs to make a few practical choices beyond the conceptual pipeline: what to use as a vector store, how to structure chunk metadata, and how to keep the ingestion and query code cleanly separated (since, as covered previously, they run at different times and different frequencies).
For a small-to-medium prototype, you don’t need a dedicated vector database server — an in-memory list of embeddings with NumPy for similarity search is often enough, and it makes the mechanics of retrieval fully visible rather than hidden behind a database client. For production scale, you’d swap this for a real vector store (e.g., a managed vector database, or a self-hosted option like FAISS or pgvector) that supports approximate nearest-neighbor search and persistence, but the surrounding pipeline code barely changes — only the storage and search calls do.
A complete minimal implementation, combining chunking, embedding, storage, retrieval, and generation into one script:
import anthropic
import voyageai
import numpy as np

vo = voyageai.Client()
client = anthropic.Anthropic()


def chunk_text(text: str, chunk_size: int = 500, overlap: int = 50) -&gt; list[str]:
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Simple fixed-size chunking with overlap, splitting on whitespace.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    words = text.split()
    chunks = []
    step = chunk_size - overlap
    for start in range(0, len(words), step):
        chunk = &quot; &quot;.join(words[start : start + chunk_size])
        if chunk:
            chunks.append(chunk)
    return chunks


class SimpleVectorStore:
    def __init__(self):
        self.chunks: list[str] = []
        self.metadata: list[dict] = []
        self.embeddings: np.ndarray | None = None

    def add_documents(self, documents: list[dict]) -&gt; None:
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Each document is {&quot;text&quot;: ..., &quot;source&quot;: ...}.&quot;&quot;&quot;
        for doc in documents:
            for chunk in chunk_text(doc[&quot;text&quot;]):
                self.chunks.append(chunk)
                self.metadata.append({&quot;source&quot;: doc[&quot;source&quot;]})

        new_embeddings = vo.embed(
            self.chunks, model=&quot;voyage-4&quot;, input_type=&quot;document&quot;
        ).embeddings
        self.embeddings = np.array(new_embeddings)

    def search(self, query: str, top_k: int = 3) -&gt; list[dict]:
        query_embedding = vo.embed(
            [query], model=&quot;voyage-4&quot;, input_type=&quot;query&quot;
        ).embeddings[0]
        scores = np.dot(self.embeddings, query_embedding)
        top_indices = np.argsort(scores)[::-1][:top_k]
        return [
            {&quot;text&quot;: self.chunks[i], &quot;source&quot;: self.metadata[i][&quot;source&quot;], &quot;score&quot;: float(scores[i])}
            for i in top_indices
        ]


def generate_answer(question: str, store: SimpleVectorStore) -&gt; str:
    results = store.search(question, top_k=3)
    context = &quot;\n\n&quot;.join(f&quot;[Source: {r[&#039;source&#039;]}]\n{r[&#039;text&#039;]}&quot; for r in results)

    response = client.messages.create(
        model=&quot;claude-opus-5&quot;,
        max_tokens=512,
        system=(
            &quot;You are a support assistant. Answer using only the provided &quot;
            &quot;documents. Cite the source of any fact you use. If the answer &quot;
            &quot;isn&#039;t in the documents, say you don&#039;t know.\n\n&quot;
            f&quot;Documents:\n{context}&quot;
        ),
        messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: question}],
    )
    return response.content[0].text


# --- Ingestion (run once, or whenever documents change) ---
store = SimpleVectorStore()
store.add_documents([
    {&quot;text&quot;: &quot;To reset your password, go to Settings &gt; Security...&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;help/password.md&quot;},
    {&quot;text&quot;: &quot;Refunds are processed within 5-7 business days after approval.&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;help/refunds.md&quot;},
])

# --- Query time (run per user request) ---
print(generate_answer(&quot;How long do refunds take?&quot;, store))
A few implementation details deserve attention. First, keeping metadata (like source) alongside each chunk from the start pays off immediately — it lets the generation step attribute facts to a document, which both improves user trust and sets up the citations feature covered elsewhere in this course. Second, notice that add_documents re-embeds all accumulated chunks every time it’s called in this simplified version; a real implementation would only embed newly added chunks and append to the existing embedding matrix, since re-embedding unchanged text wastes API calls and money. Third, error handling matters more than it looks: if the embedding API call fails partway through a large ingestion batch, you want to be able to resume rather than re-embed everything from scratch — production systems typically embed in smaller batches and track progress.
A common pitfall when first implementing this flow is testing only with a handful of documents where naive fixed-size chunking and top-3 retrieval work fine, then discovering in production that with a large, varied corpus, purely vector-based retrieval misses exact-match queries — a user searching for an exact error code or product SKU may not get a strong semantic match, because embeddings are tuned for meaning, not literal string matching. That gap is exactly what the next lesson, on BM25 lexical search, addresses.
Key takeaways

A working RAG prototype can be built with an in-memory embedding list and NumPy; production systems swap this for a persistent vector store without changing the surrounding pipeline logic.
Keep chunk-level metadata (source, section) from the start — it’s needed for attribution and later for citations.
Only re-embed new or changed chunks in production; re-embedding an entire corpus on every ingestion run is wasteful and costly.
Batch and checkpoint ingestion so a failed embedding call doesn’t force a full restart.
Pure vector search can miss exact-match queries (codes, SKUs, names) because embeddings optimize for semantic similarity, not literal matching — a gap lexical search fills.

Related lessons
