06 BM25 lexical search

claude/10-agents-and-workflows/03-chaini.content:
Chaining workflows



                  Course context 



Part of Agents and workflows — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
Chaining means breaking a big task into a series of smaller steps, where each step is its own call to Claude and feeds its answer into the next step — like an assembly line. Each individual step is simpler and easier to get right than trying to do the whole task in one giant prompt, and you can add checkpoints between steps to catch mistakes before they cascade.
Deep dive
Chaining, also called prompt chaining, is the second core workflow pattern from Anthropic’s “Building Effective Agents” guidance. Where parallelization runs independent calls concurrently, chaining runs a sequence of calls where each one depends on the last — call 2 can’t start until call 1 finishes, because call 2’s prompt is built (in part) from call 1’s output.
The motivation is decomposition. A single LLM call asked to do everything at once — say, “read this raw customer feedback, extract the themes, categorize each theme by severity, and write an executive summary” — is asking the model to juggle several distinct kinds of reasoning simultaneously. Breaking that into a chain of three or four calls (extract themes → categorize severity → draft summary → polish tone) means each individual call only has to be good at one narrower thing, which is usually easier for the model to do reliably than the combined task, and easier for a human to review and debug when something goes wrong, since you can inspect the intermediate outputs.
A defining feature of chains, distinct from a plain sequence of calls, is the gate: a check inserted between two steps that verifies the previous step’s output is acceptable before letting the chain continue. A gate can be a simple programmatic assertion (does the output parse as valid JSON? does it contain the expected fields? is it under a length limit?), a rule-based validation function, or even another LLM call whose only job is to judge the previous step’s output (“Does this summary accurately reflect the extracted themes? Answer yes or no.”). Gates are what let a chain fail fast and predictably instead of quietly passing a bad intermediate result all the way to the end, where it’s much more expensive to notice and fix.
import json
from anthropic import Anthropic

client = Anthropic()

def call(prompt: str) -&gt; str:
    response = client.messages.create(
        model=&quot;claude-opus-5&quot;,
        max_tokens=1024,
        messages=[{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: prompt}],
    )
    return response.content[0].text

def extract_themes(feedback: str) -&gt; list[str]:
    raw = call(
        f&quot;Extract the main themes from this feedback as a JSON array of strings. &quot;
        f&quot;Respond with ONLY the JSON array.\n\n{feedback}&quot;
    )
    return json.loads(raw)  # will raise if the model didn&#039;t return valid JSON

def gate_themes(themes: list[str]) -&gt; bool:
    # Simple programmatic gate: did we actually get something usable?
    return len(themes) &gt; 0 and all(isinstance(t, str) for t in themes)

def categorize_severity(themes: list[str]) -&gt; str:
    return call(
        f&quot;For each theme below, assign a severity of low/medium/high. &quot;
        f&quot;Themes: {themes}&quot;
    )

def draft_summary(severity_report: str) -&gt; str:
    return call(f&quot;Write a two-paragraph executive summary of this report:\n{severity_report}&quot;)

def run_chain(feedback: str) -&gt; str:
    themes = extract_themes(feedback)
    if not gate_themes(themes):
        raise ValueError(&quot;Theme extraction produced an unusable result — stopping chain&quot;)
    severity_report = categorize_severity(themes)
    return draft_summary(severity_report)
Each function is a link in the chain; run_chain is the fixed sequence a developer wrote, and gate_themes is the checkpoint that stops bad data from propagating into the (more expensive, harder-to-debug) later steps. In a production system the gate might retry the previous step with a corrective prompt instead of raising, but the principle — verify before proceeding — is the same.
Chaining’s core tradeoff is latency and cost versus accuracy and controllability. Each additional link in the chain adds a full round trip to the model, so a five-step chain is going to be noticeably slower and more expensive per request than a single call — but each step, being narrower in scope, is also more likely to be correct, and the intermediate outputs give you visibility you don’t get from one opaque call. The rule of thumb from the “Building Effective Agents” guidance is to add chain steps only when the task decomposition genuinely simplifies each individual step; chaining for its own sake, without a real decomposition benefit, just adds latency.
Chaining connects directly to the next lesson on routing: a chain’s first step is often itself a router — “figure out what kind of request this is” — whose output determines which of several possible next-step prompts gets used. In fact, routing can be thought of as a chain of exactly one classification step followed by a branch, rather than a fixed linear sequence; the two patterns compose naturally, and a real system often chains several steps where one of the early links is a router.
Pitfalls. The most common failure is chaining steps that don’t actually need to be separate — if step 2 never really depends on step 1’s specific content (only on the original input), you’ve paid for an extra round trip with no decomposition benefit, and the two could be merged or parallelized instead. A second pitfall is skipping gates: without a checkpoint, a malformed or wrong intermediate output silently flows downstream and corrupts everything after it, and by the time you notice the final output is wrong, it’s much harder to tell which link in the chain actually failed. A third is an unbounded chain — dynamically deciding to add “one more step” without a hard cap can turn a workflow into something that behaves like a runaway agent loop, defeating the predictability that was the whole point of choosing a workflow in the first place.
Key takeaways

Chaining decomposes one task into a fixed sequence of LLM calls, where each step’s output feeds the next step’s input.
Smaller, narrower steps are generally more accurate than one call trying to do everything, and they’re easier to debug.
Gates — programmatic checks or LLM-judge calls between steps — catch bad intermediate output before it propagates further down the chain.
The tradeoff is added latency and cost per extra link versus the accuracy and visibility gained from decomposition; only chain when decomposition genuinely helps.
A chain’s first step is often a router (next lesson) that decides which downstream path the rest of the chain should take.

