Next: 04 Routing workflows

claude/10-agents-and-workflows/05-agents.content:
Agents and tools



                  Course context 



Part of Agents and workflows — Building with the Claude API (Skilljar)



In plain terms
An agent is what you get when you give Claude a set of tools (functions it can call, like “search the web” or “read a file”) and then let it decide, on its own, which tools to use and in what order, repeating that process until it thinks the task is done. You’re no longer scripting the steps — you’re describing the goal and the available tools, and Claude works out the plan as it goes.
Deep dive
This lesson moves from workflows — where the developer fixes the sequence of LLM calls in advance — to agents, where the LLM itself drives that sequence at runtime. Per Anthropic’s “Building Effective Agents” guidance, an agent combines two ingredients: tool use (the model’s ability to request that a specific function be called, with specific arguments) and an autonomous loop that keeps running until the model decides the task is complete.
Tool use on its own is just a mechanism: you describe a set of functions to Claude (name, description, and a JSON schema for the arguments), and when Claude wants to use one, it responds with a structured request to call that function with certain arguments rather than with plain text. Your code executes the actual function, and sends the result back to Claude as part of the ongoing conversation. This mechanism by itself doesn’t make something an agent — a single workflow step can use one tool and stop. What turns tool use into agentic behavior is wrapping it in a loop that the model effectively controls: after each tool result comes back, the model decides again what to do next, and that decision — not code you wrote in advance — determines whether the process continues, uses a different tool, or wraps up.
from anthropic import Anthropic

client = Anthropic()

tools = [
    {
        &quot;name&quot;: &quot;search_docs&quot;,
        &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Search internal documentation for a query string.&quot;,
        &quot;input_schema&quot;: {
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;,
            &quot;properties&quot;: {&quot;query&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;}},
            &quot;required&quot;: [&quot;query&quot;],
        },
    },
    {
        &quot;name&quot;: &quot;send_email&quot;,
        &quot;description&quot;: &quot;Send an email to a recipient with a subject and body.&quot;,
        &quot;input_schema&quot;: {
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;,
            &quot;properties&quot;: {
                &quot;to&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;},
                &quot;subject&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;},
                &quot;body&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot;},
            },
            &quot;required&quot;: [&quot;to&quot;, &quot;subject&quot;, &quot;body&quot;],
        },
    },
]

def execute_tool(name: str, tool_input: dict) -&gt; str:
    if name == &quot;search_docs&quot;:
        return search_the_docs(tool_input[&quot;query&quot;])  # your implementation
    if name == &quot;send_email&quot;:
        return send_the_email(**tool_input)  # your implementation
    return f&quot;Unknown tool: {name}&quot;

def run_agent(task: str, max_turns: int = 10) -&gt; str:
    messages = [{&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: task}]
    for _ in range(max_turns):  # hard cap — see pitfalls below
        response = client.messages.create(
            model=&quot;claude-opus-5&quot;,
            max_tokens=1024,
            tools=tools,
            messages=messages,
        )
        if response.stop_reason != &quot;tool_use&quot;:
            # The model decided it&#039;s done — no more tool calls requested
            return next(b.text for b in response.content if b.type == &quot;text&quot;)

        messages.append({&quot;role&quot;: &quot;assistant&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: response.content})
        tool_results = []
        for block in response.content:
            if block.type == &quot;tool_use&quot;:
                result = execute_tool(block.name, block.input)
                tool_results.append({
                    &quot;type&quot;: &quot;tool_result&quot;,
                    &quot;tool_use_id&quot;: block.id,
                    &quot;content&quot;: result,
                })
        messages.append({&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: tool_results})

    return &quot;Agent did not finish within the turn limit.&quot;
Notice what’s absent from this code compared to the workflow examples in the earlier lessons: there is no branching logic that says “first search, then email.” The model looks at the task, decides on its own whether it needs to search first, whether it needs to search multiple times, whether an email is warranted at all, and when it’s done. Given the same tools, the same agent code could handle a request that needs zero tool calls, one, or a dozen, in whatever order the situation calls for — that flexibility is exactly what a fixed workflow cannot offer, and exactly what makes agents suited to open-ended tasks where the right sequence of steps genuinely can’t be known in advance.
This flexibility is also where the risk comes from. Because the model — not your code — decides how many turns to take, an agent can in principle loop far longer than expected, repeatedly calling tools without making real progress, or take an action you didn’t anticipate. That’s why the max_turns cap above isn’t optional decoration; every agent loop needs some hard stopping condition, whether that’s a turn limit, a token/cost budget, a time limit, or all three together.
Good tool design matters enormously for agent reliability. Each tool’s name and description are the only information the model has about when and how to use it — vague descriptions (“does stuff with data”) lead to the model guessing wrong about when a tool applies, while precise, example-rich descriptions (“searches internal documentation by keyword; use this before answering any question about internal policy”) measurably improve the model’s tool-selection accuracy. This is also where the next lesson picks up: an agent that can only act (call tools that change things) without any way to observe the current state of its environment is flying blind — it has to guess whether its actions worked. Environment inspection, covered next, is about giving the agent tools specifically for looking at state — reading a file listing, checking a log, querying a system’s current status — so that each decision in the loop is grounded in what’s actually true rather than what the model assumes.
Pitfalls. The single biggest risk with agents is the runaway loop: no stopping condition, or a stopping condition that’s too loose, and the agent burns tokens and time (and possibly does more real-world actions than intended) without converging. A related risk is giving an agent access to consequential tools (sending emails, making purchases, deleting data) without a human-in-the-loop confirmation step or a sandboxed/reversible environment to test in first — an agent’s dynamic decision-making means it can take an action you did not specifically anticipate. And, echoing the theme of the whole module: reaching for a full agent loop when a workflow would do is unnecessary complexity — if you can actually enumerate the steps in advance, a chain or a router is more predictable, cheaper to reason about, and usually cheaper to run than an open-ended agent loop.
Key takeaways

An agent = tool use (functions the model can request to call) + an autonomous loop where the model itself decides what happens next each turn.
Unlike a workflow, the sequence and number of steps isn’t fixed by your code — it emerges from the model’s own decisions as the task unfolds.
Tool descriptions are the model’s only information about when and how to use a tool — precise, example-rich descriptions materially improve reliability.
Every agent loop needs an explicit stopping condition (turn cap, budget, or time limit); without one, a runaway loop is a real operational risk.
Agents that can act but can’t observe their environment are guessing — the next lesson, environment inspection, covers grounding agent decisions in real state.

Related lessons
Previous: 04 Routing workflows
Next: 06 Environment inspection
